00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:29.640
AUDIENCE: With the
[INAUDIBLE] ceramic class,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.270
I wish we had got to know the
people before because we were

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.300
really friendly when
the camera was off,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.828
and they were kind of silent
when the camera was on.

00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:39.870
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, yeah.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.662
AUDIENCE: So, probably
if we went back now,

00:00:41.662 --> 00:00:44.094
it would be different.

00:00:44.094 --> 00:00:47.270
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I feel like the
boom was really intimidating

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.430
because I'd be like, oh,
like, it's in their faces,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.560
and trying to get in.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.420
You could hear their
voices just drop

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.670
and then cut off whatever
they were saying.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.710
So, yeah, I felt uncomfortable
because I came in--

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.560
like, Katarina was there for
a while before I came in.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.643
And then, as soon as I got
there, I got my camera.

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:07.393
And the first time I
was talking to people

00:01:07.393 --> 00:01:08.690
I was, like, behind it.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.370
So, I don't.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:16.580
I think I was more reserved
in what I was asking them

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.270
and how close I was
getting to them.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.400
So, I think this part is just
getting comfortable with it

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.810
and just--

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.112
then I think if I had gotten a
chance to talk to some of them,

00:01:28.112 --> 00:01:30.020
it would be better.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.580
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
the way a camera

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.110
changes the situation
is pretty amazing.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.840
And that's obviously
a huge part of what

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.160
we've been talking about.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.470
The boom is also a
big part of that.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:43.707
And as you say, especially for
people who haven't experienced

00:01:43.707 --> 00:01:46.040
that before, it can be kind
of intimidating because it's

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.500
like this weapon, right?

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.647
And the ways around that are,
as we discussed in class,

00:01:53.647 --> 00:01:55.730
you can actually attach
the directional microphone

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.890
to the top of the camera,
which at least makes

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.600
it more of a compact unit.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.358
You do sacrifice some ability
to get really good sound

00:02:03.358 --> 00:02:05.900
in certain situations because
you can't get the microphone as

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.820
close, obviously,
since it's limited

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.229
to wherever the camera is.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.960
But it does have the
advantage that there's

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.960
only one intimidating piece of
equipment in the room, not two,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.950
and it's more contained.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.280
So, that is something
that you might consider

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.600
for some kinds of situations,
and it may or may not

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.807
be appropriate.

00:02:27.807 --> 00:02:30.140
And then, of course, the other
thing is, yeah, prepping.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.798
Prepping the room, prepping
the scene, meeting the people.

00:02:34.798 --> 00:02:36.590
Again, depending on
how much time you have,

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.310
meeting them without a camera
before you take the camera out.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.300
As I think I said before,
I'll often come with a camera,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:48.260
but I won't necessarily
be committed

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.780
to the idea of taking it out
the first time I meet people

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.390
because you just have
to see how it goes.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.880
And if it seems really great
and they're very comfortable

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.380
and you make a lot
of progress, you

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.297
can say, oh, do you mind
if I shoot something?

00:02:59.297 --> 00:03:02.660
But if there is that need to
build a rapport before you

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.890
get to that point, you just
leave the camera in the bag.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.290
And then, if something
amazing is starting to happen,

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.790
you have it.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.180
But I realize also that
in an exercise situation,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.680
you're not necessarily going
to spend a week or two building

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.280
a rapport with a ceramics class
before you go out and shoot.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.810
But part of what
we're doing here

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.185
is trying to get some experience
in these different kinds

00:03:23.185 --> 00:03:26.660
of shooting, and just
get an idea of some

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.160
of the challenges involved in
the different kinds of things.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:29.840
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And one other thing.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.548
We talked about this
a little bit before,

00:03:31.548 --> 00:03:34.880
but if you did, say, have
someone with a camera,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.360
as Chris mentioned, with a
unidirectional-- the longer mic

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.680
on top, it could still be
helpful to have two people.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.180
Because in that
instance, one person

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.400
can be focusing on shooting
it, and the other person

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.650
could be focusing on just
interacting with people.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.080
And then that gives people
in the ceramics class

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.440
or whatever the situation
is somebody to focus on,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.307
as opposed to focusing on
whoever has the equipment.

00:03:58.307 --> 00:04:00.890
So, sometimes that helps people
become a lot more comfortable.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.623
One person can be in charge of
socially engaging with whoever

00:04:05.623 --> 00:04:07.790
it is that you're working
with, and the other person

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.910
can focus on the
shooting and the sound.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.200
CHRIS BOEBEL: And then,
another thing that you can do,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.742
which we don't really have the
equipment to do in this class.

00:04:15.742 --> 00:04:18.649
But if you had, for instance,
a digital audio recorder

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.810
of your own, the
other person could

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.330
carry the digital audio
recorder and a microphone,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:24.742
just handheld.

00:04:24.742 --> 00:04:26.700
And that's a little less
intimidating, as well.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.402
And you can obviously get closer
or get a different sound angle,

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:32.360
or you can record the
sound separately and then

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.430
sync it up in editing.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.770
So, that's yet another
technique that you

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:41.030
could use to get a different
kind of audio perspective

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.078
without using the boom
and being as intimidating.

00:04:43.078 --> 00:04:44.870
CHRISTINE WALLEY: If
we had more equipment.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.570
CHRIS BOEBEL: The boom is great
for some situations, obviously,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:53.210
but it does introduce another
element of distraction.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.240
So, the other thing
that I wanted to do

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.140
is talk a little bit more about
Errol Morris, just kind of wrap

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.518
that up since we were a
little truncated on Wednesday.

00:05:03.518 --> 00:05:05.060
And then we're going
to go on to talk

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.550
about this current
assignment a bit more.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.800
We're going to get a visit
from a dean, Christopher Jones,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.980
who's going to talk a little
bit about MIT students.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.680
And we're also going to talk
about interview techniques

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.350
and various things
to think about when

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.390
you're setting up an interview.

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:24.380
So, that's today.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.560
But just first, to
spend a few minutes

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.800
thinking about Errol
Morris, or wrapping that up.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.320
Final reactions or thoughts
that people didn't get a chance

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:38.551
to say to his work or to that
final film, The Fog of War?

00:05:43.238 --> 00:05:45.280
AUDIENCE: It was interesting
how he actually used

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.430
a lot of jump cuts to do it.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.191
It really is different
from normal films.

00:05:51.191 --> 00:05:53.024
You would think-- he's
a very famous person.

00:05:53.024 --> 00:05:54.780
It's not like it's something
he just put together.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.322
But I think it's interesting to
hear his own voice randomly--

00:05:57.322 --> 00:05:59.036
not randomly, but
at different points.

00:05:59.036 --> 00:06:01.680
And then you have it just jump
between thing and another.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.707
I think one of the [INAUDIBLE]
he'd fade out and then fade in.

00:06:05.707 --> 00:06:08.040
But I think in this one, he
just didn't do that as much.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.212
Or at least [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:11.212 --> 00:06:12.170
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:13.610
Sorry.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.310
AUDIENCE: I liked it because
it was a good example of what

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.220
to do with my last piece
because it was really based

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.480
on what the person was saying.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.355
And then I would cut.

00:06:21.355 --> 00:06:25.040
And so I was having
difficulty because I

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.450
didn't want it to sound like
this random voice from nowhere.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.460
And so I kept trying to
go back to the person.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:33.290
But then, after
looking at it, I was

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.180
like, oh, it's so much of her.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.100
I really wanted to show
more of the food, as well.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.750
So, I think watching him, it
was a really good balance.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:45.920
And there's actually more of
everything else than on him,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.420
but I didn't feel
like I didn't know

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.190
who he was when he was talking.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:53.150
So, I think that
helped, at least,

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.270
figure out how to
do some of that.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.580
I'm not exactly sure
how he did it, though.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:02.198
I think [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:02.198 --> 00:07:03.490
CHRIS BOEBEL: That makes sense.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:05.200
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I was
going to mention in relation

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.430
to Sabrina's comment.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:09.367
So, the focus on the jump cuts.

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:11.200
So, when we're thinking,
again, the contrast

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.870
between digital cinema style--

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.210
direct, sorry.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:20.020
Between direct cinema style
and Errol Morris's style.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.680
Again, the direct cinema
style is much more--

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.540
focusing less on the editing.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.370
Obviously, editing
is still part of it,

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.067
but less of a focus
on the editing,

00:07:32.067 --> 00:07:34.150
and you don't really want
to call attention to it.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.490
And generally you don't
call attention also

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.230
to the person who's
doing the filming.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.480
So, in both of those
cases, again, him

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.190
breaking very strongly with
direct cinema style where--

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.460
doing jump cuts.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.400
Calling attention to the fact
that this is being filmed,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.840
that this is being staged.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.770
Hearing his voice off camera.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.520
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
you can think about it

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:59.710
as, in a lot of ways, all
documentary filmmakers in one

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.770
way or another are in
conversation with this question

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.300
of the artifice of filmmaking.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.632
So, they're taking
something that is reality

00:08:07.632 --> 00:08:10.090
and they're trying to represent
it through this medium that

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.400
is about artifice
and manipulation

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.726
and the direct cinema
approach has just

00:08:14.726 --> 00:08:18.310
minimized the sense
in which that there's

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.180
a manipulation, whether or
not it's true that there is.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.200
And Errol Morris is saying, I'm
just going to put it out there.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:27.130
I'm editing.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.440
This is edited here.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.880
This is my interpretation
of what was said,

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:34.390
and I am manipulating
what was said.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:36.460
And I'm showing you
where I cut things out.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.057
And I'm not going
to worry about it.

00:08:39.057 --> 00:08:41.140
The other thing that's
interesting about his work,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.030
for better or worse,
is if you think

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:47.230
back to some of the
very early stuff we saw,

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.560
like that film on the
whirlwind computer

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:53.590
from the '50s with the voiceover
narration, the voice of God

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:56.080
narration, in some
ways, his technique

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.270
is very similar to that, except
that it's not a voice of God

00:08:59.270 --> 00:08:59.770
yet.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:03.010
The voice is really
located in the subject.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:06.010
But he, in so many
ways, is illustrating

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:10.180
what's being said with
cutaways, with found footage,

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:13.240
or various kinds of imagery.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.240
But rather than being a literal
illustration, in many cases,

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.920
it's more of a psychological
or a very, very surjective

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:22.030
illustration.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:25.480
And the music, of course,
the Philip Glass music

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:28.820
in that piece really
highlights that

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:34.240
and increases the feeling
of subjective dislocation,

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.800
if that's the right term.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.880
AUDIENCE: Would you say there is
much more planning before even

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.840
deciding to shoot a
Morris-style kind of film,

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.385
whereas with direct
cinema, there is only--

00:09:49.385 --> 00:09:54.500
I mean, how much planning should
you do before a direct cinema

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.760
experiment?

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.760
CHRIS BOEBEL: I think it's hard
to say how you would answer

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:03.620
that because it really depends
on exactly what you're doing.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.850
The planning, in the case
of an Errol Morris film,

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.530
is picking the right subject.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:12.200
Picking someone who
who will hold up

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:16.470
under that kind of intense
scrutiny as a human being,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:21.590
as a personality, who's
engaging enough to carry a film.

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:23.360
And many of his
films, obviously,

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.943
aren't carried by a
single individual.

00:10:24.943 --> 00:10:28.700
But all of them are
about people who can

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:30.650
talk in some way or another.

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:32.810
Whether or not they
can talk in the sense

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.265
of being very articulate.

00:10:35.265 --> 00:10:37.640
And, of course, McNamara is
very articulate in that film.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.317
But some of his
characters are just

00:10:39.317 --> 00:10:41.900
very compelling in other ways,
not because they're necessarily

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:44.060
so able to speak.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.590
But they're
compelling characters.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.530
And so, there's a huge
amount of pre-production

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.420
in picking the right
people to tell the story,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:57.030
and picking a story that lends
itself well to that technique.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.630
And then, after that,
it's interesting

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:03.230
because is it
pre-production work

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.640
or is it post-production work?

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.250
Because there's so
much work and attention

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.340
that has to be done to
structuring a story when

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.320
you do it like that.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.072
What are the linking bits?

00:11:14.072 --> 00:11:15.530
How do you get
people-- and this is

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.280
what we'll talk about
later in class-- how

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.360
do you get people to tell
their story in a coherent way,

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.140
or in a way that can be
made coherent in editing?

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:29.030
So, for instance, if
I'm interviewing Chris

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:31.610
and I want to get
her life history,

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.800
and I say, when were you born?

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:37.160
And she says,
1960-whatever it was.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:38.870
CHRISTINE WALLEY: 65.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:41.600
CHRIS BOEBEL: 65.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.300
I can't really use that unless
you include my question.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.912
So, I need her to say,
I was born in 1965.

00:11:47.912 --> 00:11:50.120
And then, hopefully, to make
some kind of observation

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:54.420
on why that might
even be interesting.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:55.970
So, that's what
we're going to talk

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.670
about in the last half of
class, how you actually

00:11:58.670 --> 00:11:59.600
do things like that.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.220
What kinds of basic
techniques can you

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:03.680
use to get people to
talk about themselves.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:05.548
Or about whatever you're
asking them about.

00:12:05.548 --> 00:12:07.340
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I
mean, I think probably,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:09.007
maybe what Katarina
is trying to get it.

00:12:09.007 --> 00:12:11.930
I mean, there is--

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.090
we were talking some about how
Errol Morris likes to use sets.

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:17.090
And there is discussion
in some of the readings,

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.007
for example, when he was
shooting Fast, Cheap,

00:12:19.007 --> 00:12:21.470
and Out of Control that he
did these nighttime shoots

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:22.700
with the topiary.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:25.583
And he wanted to do
it in slower speed.

00:12:25.583 --> 00:12:27.500
So, there's massive,
massive numbers of light.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:29.788
So, in that sense, yeah.

00:12:29.788 --> 00:12:31.580
I mean, a lot of emphasis
for Errol Morris,

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:34.722
depending on what
he's shooting on,

00:12:34.722 --> 00:12:36.680
creating sets, and creating
the context for how

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:37.550
he wants things to look.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:38.925
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Because again, he's

00:12:38.925 --> 00:12:41.870
not afraid of embracing the
artificial qualities of film.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.670
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And thinking
also about in Gates of Heaven

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:45.045
when he created
those portraits--

00:12:45.045 --> 00:12:47.210
I mean, obviously, when
he was shooting interviews

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:50.060
with people, he was
spending a lot of time

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.950
in the setup
beforehand, what exactly

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:53.450
do I want to have
in the background,

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:56.480
how do I frame this in a
way that's absolutely right.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.400
So, there was a great
deal of emphasis

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.070
placed on setting
up those interviews

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:02.980
before he even interviewed them.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:03.980
So, in that sense, yeah.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:07.520
I mean, there is a
lot more planning,

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:09.890
whereas the direct cinema
style being more, OK, let's

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:11.598
grab the camera,
let's hop into--

00:13:11.598 --> 00:13:12.890
ah, there's people doing stuff.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.765
Let's just hop right in
and follow them along

00:13:14.765 --> 00:13:15.728
and do whatever.

00:13:15.728 --> 00:13:16.520
So, in that sense--

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:18.353
CHRIS BOEBEL: In direct
cinema, the planning

00:13:18.353 --> 00:13:20.450
can involve access.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:23.570
Think about primary.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.820
I don't know the story,
but it may or may not

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.830
have been easy to get
the kind of access

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.680
that they needed in order to
follow Humphrey and Kennedy.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:32.060
Or any kind of film.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.310
I mean, obviously, there's
a lot of negotiation that

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:37.190
has to go on before you
can even be in the room

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:42.107
with people that are
free to be themselves,

00:13:42.107 --> 00:13:43.940
and that would allow
you to actually capture

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:46.850
something direct cinema style.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:47.820
So, it can vary.

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.950
But yeah, I mean,
certainly in terms

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:53.280
of the experience
of filming, one

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.490
is about spontaneity and being--

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.380
well, they both can
be about spontaneity.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.570
But one is about being
freewheeling and not

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:04.858
trying to direct, and
embrace this artifice,

00:14:04.858 --> 00:14:06.900
the plastic elements of
film that we talked about

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:07.710
at the beginning.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:10.800
And the other is very much
like, well, that's what film is.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.590
Why not?

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.360
So, at 11:00,
we're going to have

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.230
a visit from Dean
Christopher Jones, who's

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:22.080
going to talk a little
bit about MIT students.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:25.530
And just to prep
for that, I wanted

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:29.040
to talk a bit about the
second part of the assignment.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.740
As I said, I'm encouraging
you for the second part

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:40.020
of this exercise, which is
an interview-based exercise,

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:47.160
I'm encouraging your teams
to interview an MIT student.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:52.960
And this may become part of a
project that I'm working on,

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:58.950
which is essentially a profile
of the MIT student body, which

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:01.860
is going to be shown
in various events

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.140
as part of the MIT
150 celebration that's

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:05.740
happening right now.

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:09.750
Including possibly before
this big convocation

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:12.180
that's happening at the Boston
Convention Center in April.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:13.598
So, it's kind of fun.

00:15:13.598 --> 00:15:14.640
It could be kind of cool.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.060
And what I'm interested
in is the idea

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.400
of how MIT students
look at MIT students.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.690
Both graduate and
undergraduate, no distinction.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.480
Although, obviously,
there is a difference.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:32.970
And I'm just kind of
interested in the question of,

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.621
is there a typical MIT student?

00:15:36.621 --> 00:15:41.850
Are there characteristics
that cross

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:48.480
traditional boundaries of
ethnicity, or gender, or age,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.665
or interest?

00:15:52.686 --> 00:15:55.920
It's a truism to say that
MIT students are just

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:57.660
an incredibly
diverse lot when it

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.410
comes to their interests
and their goals

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:02.550
and their aims in life.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:04.133
And I'm just interested
in seeing what

00:16:04.133 --> 00:16:06.220
MIT students think about that.

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.040
So, I would strongly
encourage you,

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:12.900
if you're at all interested,
to participate in that.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:17.790
And Dean Jones is going to talk
a little bit about strategies

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:20.140
for that, as well.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:21.960
And I'm also-- the
second part of this

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:25.170
is, I would very much love
to have the opportunity

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:29.160
to film you filming.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:33.960
So, if you're able to do
this and you actually want

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:35.910
to profile an MIT student--

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.180
again, grad or undergrad-- and
it's very open as to who you

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:43.950
might choose, I would love to
be able to come and spend a very

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:44.922
short amount of time--

00:16:44.922 --> 00:16:46.380
not the entire time
because we know

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.270
how cameras can influence
those kinds of things.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:51.990
But I would love to come capture
just a little bit of actually

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.180
doing the exercise.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:57.660
We can talk about this
offline or separately.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:02.130
But over the next week, I would
love to try to, if possible,

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:06.210
coordinate schedules to do that.

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:10.650
So, also in advance
of the exercise,

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:17.460
I wanted to show you a film
that was made here at MIT,

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:22.140
or show you a few minutes of
a film that was made in 1957--

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.599
maybe some of you have seen
it, although probably not--

00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:26.880
called The Social Beaver.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.620
Anybody heard of this film?

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:41.070
Which is a very
interesting example of how

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:44.850
MIT views itself, or not.

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:50.640
You'll recognize some
of the techniques

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.190
that we've discussed in
class, like especially

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:54.440
in the first part of class.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:56.940
But I'm just going to show the
first seven or eight minutes,

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.400
and then we can talk
a little bit about it.

00:18:09.667 --> 00:18:10.334
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:18:10.334 --> 00:18:13.813
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:13.780
- The Charles River in
Massachusetts separates Boston

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:16.420
from the famous University
City of Cambridge.

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:19.300
Here, stretching for a
mile along the north bank,

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.970
is the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology,

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:25.030
known the world over as MIT.

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:27.850
This famous university
offers to its students

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:31.930
intellectual challenge
together with fellowship.

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:37.090
MIT, founded in 1861 by William
Barton Rogers, a distinguished

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:40.090
natural scientist,
has, for a century,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.710
been the leader
in the development

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:43.690
of science and technology.

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:45.910
Today, devoted to
the highest goals

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:49.630
of education and research,
MIT is a new type

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.670
of educational institution.

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:55.930
A modern University built around
science and technology rather

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:58.180
than classical
studies, but embracing

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:03.460
the arts, the social
sciences, and the humanities.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:05.860
The campus contains 100 acres.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.050
The main educational
group of buildings,

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:10.060
centered around
the Great Court, is

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:12.460
a single, continuous
structure, although it

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:14.830
is divided into numbered
sections referred

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.200
to as buildings.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.930
MIT's campus is
all inclusive with

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.170
an extensive athletic plant
and playing fields fully

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:24.200
accessible.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.260
So are the
recreational buildings,

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:28.720
dormitories, and dining halls.

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:32.820
It is a pleasant place in
which to work and live.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:50.580
The dormitory system permits
students of all classes

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:52.785
to live in any of the
undergraduate houses.

00:20:56.570 --> 00:21:00.020
The Dorrance Laboratory houses
food technology and biology,

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:04.640
and is the leader in biophysical
and biochemical research.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:08.450
The swimming pool and squash
courts occupy this building.

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:10.610
The Institute provides
this beautiful home

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:13.000
for its presidents.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.910
Walker Memorial houses a
large student dining hall

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:19.680
and is the student
activities center.

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:21.780
The Charles Haden
Memorial Library

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:25.590
is the center of the
Institute library system.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.440
The facilities for
study at MIT are fine,

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.450
but these able and
vigorous students

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:33.090
need many extracurricular
activities

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:36.360
to complete their development
for future communal, family,

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:38.340
and social relationships.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:42.660
Symbolized by the mascot beaver,
engineer of the animal world,

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:46.320
the active student after classes
is indeed the social beaver,

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.440
for MIT offers him
complete fulfillment.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:52.338
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.590
A large student leaving group
resides in the dormitories

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:00.550
and enjoys a type
of community living.

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:02.980
Roommates work
together harmoniously.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:28.950
Apartments are provided in
each dormitory for a faculty

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.830
resident and his wife, who
live among the students

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:34.410
and open their apartments
to them for evening bull

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:38.820
sessions, snacks, and
general sociability.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.610
Numerous lounges are
available to the students.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.610
Dormitory living provides
unlimited opportunities

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:47.070
for working and
playing together.

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:49.050
Throughout the
Institute's living groups,

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.690
emphasis is placed
on the individual.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:55.260
Yet each student is exposed
to many different philosophies

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:55.905
and ideas.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:00.220
Chess is popular.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:03.430
There are rooms for ping
pong, billiards, cards, music,

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:05.470
and photography.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:07.090
Dorm parties are unique.

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.100
This is driftwood drag.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:12.058
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:20.340
Dormitory council governs
the dormitory system.

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:23.190
Each house has its own
committee to plan dances,

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:26.340
student-faculty coffee
hours, and intramural sports.

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:35.500
MIT has over 25 local and
national fraternities,

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.360
each with its own house.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:39.940
In this leaving group,
informal bull sessions

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:42.070
are always taking place,
with the talk ranging

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:45.720
from nuclear fission to girls.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.850
Each house must,
of necessity, offer

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:51.060
the atmosphere and conditions
conducive to study.

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:53.640
Each fraternity member is
provided with a quiet study

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:56.590
area, an adequate
desk, and, of course,

00:23:56.590 --> 00:23:59.430
the inevitable hi-fi
set is close by.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:02.445
Mutual aid in studies is
sought and cheerfully given.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:08.510
Every fraternity has a
full slate of officers

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:11.180
and holds periodic
meetings of its members.

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:15.650
Each house conducts its
affairs in businesslike manner.

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:17.630
Before the freshman
quizzes, it is

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:20.270
customary in most
houses for upperclassmen

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:21.905
to give formal review sessions.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:33.070
About 100 coeds attend MIT
and participate in all aspects

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:34.390
of student affairs.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.250
The girls have their
own social rooms

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:39.800
in the main school building.

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:43.610
Inside this door, it's
strictly no men allowed.

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:46.040
Here the coeds congregate
between classes

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:48.500
and relax from the
pressure of class work.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:51.800
Among other things, this is the
girls' communication center.

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.110
In typical feminine
fashion, the girls

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:58.100
take time out to gossip about
clothes and dates exchange news

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:00.818
and congratulate or
sympathize with one another

00:25:00.818 --> 00:25:01.610
about their grades.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.530
MIT coeds are gifted
musically, too, and relax

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:10.780
in this enjoyable
manner after finishing

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:14.030
with gossiping and other talk.

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:15.800
When studying in
the horizontal, one

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:19.040
must be prepared to
accept this consequence.

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:20.810
The Margaret Cheney
room is actually

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.180
a beautifully appointed
suite of rooms.

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:25.160
Besides the large
living room, there

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:27.590
is a locker room,
fully equipped kitchen,

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:29.480
and this special study room.

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:31.790
Here the girls study
in complete silence,

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.595
and the signs of intense
concentration are evident.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:40.560
Well, most of the time anyway.

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:44.070
After all, the studios beaver
is also the social beaver.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.680
And what is more natural
than planning and daydreaming

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:48.165
about that new evening gown?

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:54.160
The women's dormitory
at 120 Bay State Road

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:58.370
is comfortable and homelike.

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:00.710
Here the girls share
in the work and play

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:03.230
that is typical of
girls' dorms everywhere.

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:12.034
On a weekend, the living room
is likely to look like this.

00:26:12.034 --> 00:26:15.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:28.817 --> 00:26:29.400
[END PLAYBACK]

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:30.380
CHRIS BOEBEL: So.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:31.322
Anyway.

00:26:31.322 --> 00:26:32.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:40.070
So, that's how MIT
represented itself in 1957.

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:40.570
Thoughts?

00:26:44.755 --> 00:26:46.533
CHRISTINE WALLEY: She doesn't.

00:26:46.533 --> 00:26:48.450
Need some space to go
relax from the pressure?

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:50.426
[LAUGHTER]

