know where it was,

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:54.390
it was kind of weird when
you kind of know where it is.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:57.750
[INAUDIBLE]

00:27:57.750 --> 00:28:01.110
AUDIENCE: What was it shot?

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.040
CHRIS BOEBEL: It
came out in 1957.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.830
So I assume it was shot around
that time or right before,

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:07.380
yeah.

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:12.870
It was produced by an alum
from the class of 1922.

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:18.960
So, he was already in his
late 50s when he made this.

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:20.798
That's an interesting question.

00:28:20.798 --> 00:28:23.340
Would the students of that time
have thought about this film?

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.790
I mean, it was obviously
pretty typical in terms

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:30.250
of its technique and
its style for the time.

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:33.330
So it's not that
it would have been

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:36.060
seen as so outrageous the
way we might look at it,

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:39.665
but I would have been curious
to know what the current student

00:28:39.665 --> 00:28:41.040
body would have
thought about it.

00:28:44.127 --> 00:28:45.960
AUDIENCE: Well, basically,
it doesn't really

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.860
say anything about the social
life of students at MIT.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:53.220
It's a promotional media about
how great MIT students are.

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:56.130
But do we see any
evidence of this?

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:57.030
No.

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:58.380
CHRIS BOEBEL: I mean, and it's--

00:28:58.380 --> 00:28:58.880
yeah.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:02.010
I mean, it's obviously
this voice of God narration

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:04.020
that we talked about at
the beginning of class,

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:05.478
and then everything
is illustrated.

00:29:05.478 --> 00:29:10.520
But even the things that
they choose to focus on are--

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:13.070
it's all infrastructure.

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:15.420
The women's dorm is
at 120 Bay State Road.

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:20.948
Here's the special lounge.

00:29:20.948 --> 00:29:22.740
Here's this building,
here's this building,

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:24.280
here's this building.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:24.780
So, yeah.

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:28.800
It's very interesting that
the social life of the student

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:34.263
is kind of all communicated
via infrastructure.

00:29:34.263 --> 00:29:35.805
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But then, I think--

00:29:35.805 --> 00:29:39.030
I mean, the same way that
MIT does now, I mean,

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.890
so much of this
is intended to try

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:44.843
to counter other stereotypes
about science and engineering

00:29:44.843 --> 00:29:46.260
students to be
completely asocial.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.552
I mean, that's the whole point.

00:29:47.552 --> 00:29:50.670
And so deliberately
showing shots of--

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:56.130
I mean, most of the shots,
the students being social.

00:29:56.130 --> 00:30:00.570
But it's also a lot of
young men and young women

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:04.770
together, I mean,
with the intent

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:07.590
of deliberately trying to
counter other stereotypes that

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:10.153
would have--

00:30:10.153 --> 00:30:11.820
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
just the fact that--

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:18.822
I mean, as retro
and obviously sexist

00:30:18.822 --> 00:30:20.280
as that portrayal
is, just the fact

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:23.260
that there's so much emphasis on
the fact that women attend MIT.

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:23.760
Because--

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:25.350
AUDIENCE: Yes, but how
many women [INAUDIBLE]??

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:26.140
CHRIS BOEBEL: Only 100.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:26.940
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, only 100.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.590
CHRIS BOEBEL: But they were
proud of the fact at that point

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:31.048
that there are 100 because if
you go back five or six years

00:30:31.048 --> 00:30:32.418
before there were like, 17.

00:30:32.418 --> 00:30:33.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And before that, it

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:36.528
was really MIT was
the men's school here,

00:30:36.528 --> 00:30:38.820
and its "partner school,"
quote unquote, was Wellesley.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:40.170
That's why there's still
this ongoing connection

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:40.795
with Wellesley.

00:30:40.795 --> 00:30:45.810
So, that was the idea, was
that the MIT students would--

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.075
so social events there were
between Wellesley and MIT.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.363
AUDIENCE: I wonder if
they were trying to--

00:30:56.363 --> 00:30:58.890
because they show the girls
doing some more silly stuff

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.050
than the guys doing.

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:05.190
But you wonder how much of that
was trying to say, oh, look,

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:08.555
they're normal, or
trying to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:08.555 --> 00:31:10.430
CHRIS BOEBEL: I think
they were trying to say

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:12.020
they're normal, yeah, I agree.

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:12.680
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But, as you say,

00:31:12.680 --> 00:31:14.763
I mean, the language and
the first thing that they

00:31:14.763 --> 00:31:16.400
say coming into--
oh, here's the space

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:18.450
where the young women go
to relax from the pressure.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:19.992
I mean, that's not
what they say when

00:31:19.992 --> 00:31:22.880
they introduce the young
guys going into their dorms.

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:25.040
I mean, it is pretty--

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:26.720
AUDIENCE: Fall
asleep and crying.

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:28.220
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, they gossip.

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:30.387
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] while
the guys study together.

00:31:30.387 --> 00:31:30.910
[INAUDIBLE]

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:31.910
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Sorry?

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:34.532
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:37.742
CHRISTINE WALLEY: That, yeah.

00:31:37.742 --> 00:31:39.200
AUDIENCE: They
never even mentioned

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:42.350
any type of study they did
at all, whereas the guys were

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:45.200
like, oh look, they're studying
engineering and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:46.610
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, yeah.

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:49.630
Well, I mean, they're walking
a fine line here because I'm

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:52.130
sure they're very aware that a
lot of the audience is going,

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.122
why would women
go to MIT at all?

00:31:54.122 --> 00:31:56.330
What are they doing studying
engineering and science?

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:58.160
My goodness, that seems wrong.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.490
So they're trying to make
it like, oh, but there's

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:03.650
this friendly, home-like
environment where

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:06.050
they live, and they have this
special lounge where they

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:07.633
can relax from the pressure.

00:32:07.633 --> 00:32:09.050
But at the same
time, they're also

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:11.840
aware that another
segment of their audience

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:14.240
is saying, even
at that time, it's

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:17.990
outrageous that only
100 women attend MIT.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:22.970
And so they're trying
to be very political,

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:28.063
and not succeeding very well.

00:32:28.063 --> 00:32:29.480
AUDIENCE: So when
did the walker--

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:32.150
I mean, when did the
student activity center

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:33.905
move to the ugly building?

00:32:33.905 --> 00:32:35.675
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:35.675 --> 00:32:36.800
CHRIS BOEBEL: I don't know.

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:37.550
Where was that built?

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:38.670
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, one of
the new student [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:39.860
I don't know.

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:40.400
Don't know.

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:40.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: I don't know.

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:42.030
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
I mean, one question--

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:43.940
I mean, I'm not
sure exactly if this

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:45.732
was one of the directions
you wanted to go,

00:32:45.732 --> 00:32:47.457
but thinking about
how diversity at MIT

00:32:47.457 --> 00:32:49.040
would have been
presented at this time

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:50.665
and how it would be
conceptualized now.

00:32:50.665 --> 00:32:53.510
I mean, so here, obviously,
the only diversity here is,

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:54.650
OK, there are women here.

00:32:54.650 --> 00:33:02.630
And it is completely presented
in a heterosexually oriented

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:05.480
kind of frame where that's the
assumption, that this is, OK,

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:08.630
we've got women here so
you guys can hook up,

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:10.712
kind of is the assumption
built into there.

00:33:10.712 --> 00:33:12.170
And there's not
discussion in terms

00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:13.337
of other kinds of diversity.

00:33:13.337 --> 00:33:18.570
There's not discussion in
terms of class diversity,

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:22.550
ethnic and racial
diversity, sexual diversity.

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:24.590
I mean, so there's a very--

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:27.537
how would diversity on
campus been conceptualized

00:33:27.537 --> 00:33:29.120
at that point in
terms of how would it

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:31.990
be conceptualized now in terms
of how people think about,

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:33.740
well, what entails a
diverse student body?

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:40.370
And what would be considered
to be the ideal composition

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:44.888
of the MIT student body?

00:33:44.888 --> 00:33:50.390
AUDIENCE: They do mention class
diversity, so in the dorm,

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:51.980
students of all
classes [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:52.910
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But I thought-- see,

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:53.630
I did the first thing, too.

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:54.830
I thought they were
talking about social class,

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:56.413
but I think they
meant of all classes,

00:33:56.413 --> 00:33:58.670
like freshman, sophomore,
junior, senior.

00:33:58.670 --> 00:34:00.590
It wasn't about social classes.

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:02.100
It was--

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:06.195
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
Harvard, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:06.195 --> 00:34:07.070
CHRISTINE WALLEY: No.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:09.080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:10.034 --> 00:34:10.909
CHRISTINE WALLEY: No.

00:34:10.909 --> 00:34:12.710
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, there there
was that feeling at the time.

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:14.030
I think Chris is
right that that's

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:15.238
what they were talking about.

00:34:15.238 --> 00:34:20.150
But MIT, particularly at
that time, but continuing,

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:23.690
has always seen itself as
an institution in comparison

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:27.980
to, say, the Ivy Leagues where
social class matters, does not

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:31.070
matter, and where
it's a meritocracy.

00:34:31.070 --> 00:34:32.780
That's the self-perception.

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:36.440
And it doesn't necessarily
inform this film.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:39.830
I think that, as Chris said,
that what's informing this film

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:43.730
is this need, number one, to
show off the infrastructure,

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:45.770
which is really interesting.

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:50.870
And number two, to counter
stereotypes of MIT students

00:34:50.870 --> 00:34:55.610
as people who have no social
lives, study all the time.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:57.590
This whole issue that
I mentioned of the fact

00:34:57.590 --> 00:34:59.540
that they want to
brag about the fact

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:01.940
that there are women
there, women at MIT.

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:05.690
But they're also worried
about alumni who are probably,

00:35:05.690 --> 00:35:07.940
at that time, outraged
that they've now

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:11.240
let in over 100 women.

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:14.915
So, that is the overriding
purpose of the film.

00:35:18.116 --> 00:35:19.850
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
it doesn't--

00:35:19.850 --> 00:35:23.140
it shows social life as
in student activities,

00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:25.670
but it doesn't really
show social life

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:27.890
as people that are working.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:31.220
So students that are in the
lab, or taking lunch break,

00:35:31.220 --> 00:35:33.350
or even talking as they are.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:42.085
I just wonder what kind of
choices were finally made.

00:35:42.085 --> 00:35:43.210
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, again--

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.508
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:45.508 --> 00:35:47.800
CHRIS BOEBEL: There there
are two things going on here.

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:50.958
Both are very interesting, and
both relate to this exercise

00:35:50.958 --> 00:35:52.000
that we're talking about.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:54.980
I mean, one is the
stylistic choices.

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.890
And that is partly just
a product of the time.

00:35:57.890 --> 00:36:02.650
So, the way of making a
documentary at that point

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:04.600
is basically thinking
of it as a newsreel.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:08.080
Write a script that says what
you want to say, and then

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:09.460
illustrate the script.

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:11.050
Go out with a
camera and a tripod

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:15.160
and frame up a bunch of shots
that you have on a list,

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:19.780
and then write it to the script.

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:23.020
So, there isn't necessarily
a sense of spontaneity

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:27.250
or these kinds of issues that
we've been talking about,

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:29.890
how you get at other
kinds of things,

00:36:29.890 --> 00:36:36.130
either via interviews or via
cinema verite, direct cinema

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:39.070
kinds of approaches.

00:36:39.070 --> 00:36:40.250
So that's one thing.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:44.140
But then, yeah,
the other thing is,

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:46.270
how do you represent
yourself, and how do you

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:54.070
get at the reality of what a
particular group of people is?

00:36:54.070 --> 00:37:00.520
I mean, genre and
style aside, it's

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:04.990
very, very easy to make the
equivalent of this film now.

00:37:04.990 --> 00:37:08.577
It's just that it wouldn't look
like that or sound like that.

00:37:08.577 --> 00:37:10.660
And one of the things that
I'm interested in doing

00:37:10.660 --> 00:37:16.450
is trying to get you guys to
participate a little bit so

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:19.120
that this is not
quite like that.

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.510
And, obviously,
what we make now is

00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:27.440
more palatable to 21st
century eyes and ears.

00:37:27.440 --> 00:37:34.900
But there's still this whole
question of representation--

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:37.180
"top-down representation,"
quote unquote--

00:37:37.180 --> 00:37:39.370
or people who
represent themselves.

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:41.950
I mean, it's like
Indigenous media.

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:43.870
[CHUCKLES]

00:37:43.870 --> 00:37:46.750
Well, we've been having
a great conversation

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:50.020
about diversity and the
MIT educational experience.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:51.760
Diversity in its many forms.

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:53.885
And it's been
really interesting.

00:37:53.885 --> 00:37:54.760
So, you can kind of--

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:55.630
CHRISTOPHER JONES: I'm
ready to be educated.

00:37:55.630 --> 00:37:56.620
CHRIS BOEBEL: You could
give your perspective,

00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.780
and then, yeah, just
continue the conversation

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:00.290
we've been having.

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:03.310
But for those of you who
don't know Christopher,

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:07.815
he's the assistant dean
for graduate students.

00:38:07.815 --> 00:38:09.190
Here's a graduate
degree from MIT

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:12.430
himself, and has been
involved for a long time

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:15.040
in a number of programs, such
as the MIT Summer Research

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:16.600
Program, MSRP.

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:18.880
The Amgen Scholars Program.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:20.162
I know I'm missing a ton.

00:38:20.162 --> 00:38:21.370
CHRISTOPHER JONES: That's OK.

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:23.037
CHRIS BOEBEL: But he
spends a great deal

00:38:23.037 --> 00:38:26.560
of time thinking about the
makeup of the graduate student

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:31.672
body here and actively
working to recruit students.

00:38:31.672 --> 00:38:32.630
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Yes.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:35.557
CHRIS BOEBEL: So, I thought it
would be a great thing for him

00:38:35.557 --> 00:38:38.140
to come in and talk a little bit
about some of the stuff we've

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:39.380
just been talking about.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:41.922
And just to give you a recap,
we talked about the assignment.

00:38:41.922 --> 00:38:44.230
And then we watched
eight minutes

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:48.610
of a film that was made
in the '50s here on MIT

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:50.350
students, which is quite funny.

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:50.755
CHRISTOPHER JONES: I bet it was.

00:38:50.755 --> 00:38:51.300
CHRIS BOEBEL: If
you can imagine.

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:51.882
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:51.882 --> 00:38:53.590
CHRISTOPHER JONES: On
many levels, right?

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:55.085
CHRIS BOEBEL: Exactly, yeah.

00:38:55.085 --> 00:38:57.460
And we were just having a
really interesting conversation

00:38:57.460 --> 00:39:01.840
about, as I said, diversity.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:06.130
Not just the traditional
measures of diversity, but also

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:08.110
academic diversity.

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:11.050
So, if you want to speak for
a couple of minutes, and then

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:12.425
we can continue
the conversation.

00:39:12.425 --> 00:39:12.790
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Sure.

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:13.690
Yeah, I'd love to.

00:39:13.690 --> 00:39:16.510
I think the richness of it is
going to be the conversation.

00:39:16.510 --> 00:39:17.285
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Would
you prefer to stand,

00:39:17.285 --> 00:39:18.280
or would you like a chair?

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:19.280
CHRISTOPHER JONES: I
don't know, what's better?

00:39:19.280 --> 00:39:20.500
What do you all usually do?

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:21.580
What do you want?

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:22.630
I can stand, I can sit.

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:24.380
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Whatever you want to do.

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:25.880
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
whatever you prefer.

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:27.520
CHRISTOPHER JONES: I'll
stand up for a little bit

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:29.050
since I've been sitting all day.

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:30.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
I'll keep this over here

00:39:30.130 --> 00:39:31.338
in case you change your mind.

00:39:31.338 --> 00:39:32.640
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Cool, cool.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:34.390
I've actually been
sitting all night, too.

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:36.010
You have to excuse me.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:37.960
So, I don't if you
mentioned, but I'm

00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:41.110
also in a PhD program
here, in addition

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:42.280
to being assistant dean.

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:45.160
And I have a problem
set due today.

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:48.010
And I took a nap at
3 o'clock, and then

00:39:48.010 --> 00:39:50.320
got back up to work on it.

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:53.322
So, I apologize if I'm
a little bit off today.

00:39:53.322 --> 00:39:54.530
But it's good to see you all.

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:55.990
Thank you for inviting me.

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:59.140
When I heard about the
project, I was really excited

00:39:59.140 --> 00:40:00.500
for a number of reasons.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:03.160
One, because I think
it's always great,

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:05.020
one of the wonderful
things about MIT

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:12.580
is its ability to get out of
the way of student innovation.

00:40:12.580 --> 00:40:14.320
So, to really move
out of the way

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:16.310
and help to facilitate
student innovation.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:19.750
And I think this is
an example of that.

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:22.330
I think we really
believe in the strength

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:24.515
and the power of the students.

00:40:24.515 --> 00:40:26.140
So, I wanted to say
a couple of things,

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:28.690
and then I'd really want
to hear more about all

00:40:28.690 --> 00:40:30.850
and engage in a
dialogue, if that's OK.

00:40:30.850 --> 00:40:33.520
And I think the
things that I'll say

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:36.970
are centered around a
question that Chris asked me

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:40.720
a while ago, and that was,
what is a typical MIT student?

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:42.850
I don't if you've had
that conversation.

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:43.990
CHRIS BOEBEL: I raised
that as a question,

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:44.580
but have not really talked--

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:45.490
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Any answers?

00:40:45.490 --> 00:40:46.330
CHRIS BOEBEL: We
didn't get there yet.

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:47.997
CHRISTOPHER JONES:
You didn't get there?

00:40:47.997 --> 00:40:50.710
Anyone want to
throw out anything?

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.720
Do you believe there is
a typical MIT student?

00:40:57.310 --> 00:40:59.780
AUDIENCE: I don't know.

00:40:59.780 --> 00:41:02.890
I was trying to think of
how I would describe it,

00:41:02.890 --> 00:41:07.775
and I can't really put a gender
on it, or a race, or a class.

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:15.633
Primarily, they're a science
or engineering student that--

00:41:15.633 --> 00:41:17.550
CHRISTOPHER JONES: But
not exclusively, right?

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:17.910
AUDIENCE: What?

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:19.470
CHRISTOPHER JONES: But
not exclusively, right?

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:20.790
AUDIENCE: Not exclusively.

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:23.690
And having a diverse
set of interests.

00:41:23.690 --> 00:41:24.840
A little eccentric.

00:41:27.678 --> 00:41:28.624
Intuitive.

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:33.500
Mostly their personality
traits [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:33.500 --> 00:41:34.500
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Yeah.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:43.570
So, I think I think one of
your challenges is going to be,

00:41:43.570 --> 00:41:47.020
in the midst of such diversity--

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:49.995
we're from all over the world,
at least at the graduate level.

00:41:49.995 --> 00:41:51.370
At the undergraduate
level, there

00:41:51.370 --> 00:41:56.062
are about 8% international
student, 8% or 9%

00:41:56.062 --> 00:41:57.020
international students.

00:41:57.020 --> 00:41:59.442
At the graduate level, about
40% international students.

00:41:59.442 --> 00:42:01.900
So there are a lot of people
from a lot of different places

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:02.980
here.

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:06.550
And we have all sorts of
interests and background.

00:42:06.550 --> 00:42:08.650
People that love jousting.

00:42:08.650 --> 00:42:10.000
People that love baking.

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:11.710
People that love
ballroom dancing.

00:42:11.710 --> 00:42:15.910
People that love sitting and
watching sitcoms all day long.

00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:18.010
After they finish their
homework, of course.

00:42:18.010 --> 00:42:19.880
So, we have a huge
range of interests.

00:42:19.880 --> 00:42:21.963
And I think part of your
challenge is going to be,

00:42:21.963 --> 00:42:25.810
in the midst of all
of that, is there

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:29.560
a common thread
that ties what seems

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:33.160
to be very different
individuals together?

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:35.710
And I think, for me
at least, thinking

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:39.550
about this particular project,
if there's a way for you all

00:42:39.550 --> 00:42:44.980
to pull out the common
thread while not negating

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:48.670
or missing the
individuality, then

00:42:48.670 --> 00:42:51.530
I think that will enhance
the work that you do.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:55.330
So, to get back to the
question, and Chris

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:58.210
and I have talked about
it a little bit before,

00:42:58.210 --> 00:42:59.980
I think there are some--

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:03.590
I think MIT students
are very different.

00:43:03.590 --> 00:43:09.250
However, there is a least common
denominator, if you would.

00:43:09.250 --> 00:43:12.465
Because I get the question
all the time, part of my job

00:43:12.465 --> 00:43:13.840
is to go out and
recruit students

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:15.800
to MIT at the graduate level.

00:43:15.800 --> 00:43:18.550
And so everyone's asking, well,
what's an MIT student like?

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:20.170
Is there a typical MIT student?

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:22.870
What's the experience
like at MIT?

00:43:22.870 --> 00:43:24.040
What do people do there?

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:25.630
What makes someone special?

00:43:25.630 --> 00:43:27.430
I do think there is an X factor.

00:43:27.430 --> 00:43:29.050
I think there's a secret sauce.

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:31.300
And to me, the secret
sauce is in the least

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:33.680
common denominator, and it's
made up of a few things.

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:36.940
One, and it kind of captures
what you were saying,

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:43.480
but one is this
unapologetic passion.

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:46.943
Most people at MIT are just
passionate about something.

00:43:46.943 --> 00:43:49.360
Now, you may not care about
what they're passionate about.

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:52.000
You may have never heard of
what they're passionate about.

00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:53.620
You may not necessarily
agree with it.

00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:55.240
But I think if you
walk around the campus,

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:57.160
you'll notice that
people are really, really

00:43:57.160 --> 00:44:00.790
passionate about something.

00:44:00.790 --> 00:44:02.980
Never in my life had
I been to any campus

00:44:02.980 --> 00:44:07.630
and I'd see somebody walking
around in a purple flight suit.

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:09.610
But you get to MIT,
and after a while

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:11.410
you see somebody
walking around or riding

00:44:11.410 --> 00:44:14.560
on a unicycle in a purple
flight suit while juggling,

00:44:14.560 --> 00:44:17.530
and you just walk
by like it's normal.

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:22.150
That person is passionate about
unicycles, purple fly suits,

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:23.230
and juggling.

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:25.070
And guarantee that
everyone in this room

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:26.320
is passionate about something.

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:33.700
I think the other thing is
this idea of an immense drive.

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:36.010
And you see it--

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:38.180
you really, really see
it in the students.

00:44:38.180 --> 00:44:39.400
And I'm sure--

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:40.345
I know one of you all.

00:44:40.345 --> 00:44:41.720
I don't know the
rest of you all.

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:44.440
But if you all are like
the one that I do know,

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:46.600
then I know you all
have an immense drive.

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:51.230
Meaning despite the
challenges that come up,

00:44:51.230 --> 00:44:53.710
whether it's academic,
cultural, social,

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:55.703
you really drive
towards those things

00:44:55.703 --> 00:44:57.370
that you're passionate
about, and you're

00:44:57.370 --> 00:44:59.050
willing to overcome
the barriers.

00:44:59.050 --> 00:45:02.110
MIT students are
involved in going

00:45:02.110 --> 00:45:08.530
to Haiti, in the turmoil
in the Middle East now.

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:09.580
Real world problems.

00:45:09.580 --> 00:45:11.230
While at the same
time being involved

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:17.260
in coming up with new
programs for our new systems

00:45:17.260 --> 00:45:20.170
to allow computers to
interact with humans.

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:21.380
You name it.

00:45:21.380 --> 00:45:25.280
So, there's this drive to
create and to learn and to do.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:28.810
And I think there
is one other factor.

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:30.277
And again, I'm
forgetting it, and I

00:45:30.277 --> 00:45:31.360
don't remember what it is.

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:33.650
But it'll come out
in the conversation.

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:36.100
So, I say that to
say, I personally

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:38.140
believe that there are
some common elements

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:39.700
among MIT students.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:41.068
And I would love--

00:45:41.068 --> 00:45:43.610
because I really want to see
the work that comes out of this,

00:45:43.610 --> 00:45:45.760
and I would love
to see how you all

00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:52.120
manage to, while highlighting
the individuality,

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:58.365
manage to find that common
thread amongst students at MIT.

00:45:58.365 --> 00:45:59.740
I think the other
thing I'll say,

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:02.330
and then I would love to hear
what you all were talking about

00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:11.870
and to get some dialogue,
is I wanted to underscore

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:15.680
the significance and importance
of the work that you're doing.

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:21.248
We go about our life without
thinking about our life often.

00:46:21.248 --> 00:46:22.790
We go about our life
without thinking

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:24.620
about the relationships
that we have.

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:29.570
And one of the most important
components of our life

00:46:29.570 --> 00:46:32.610
is relationships.

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:36.600
And the more we understand and
can appreciate the differences

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:39.000
of individuals-- because
it's easy to find somebody

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:40.292
that you're common with, right?

00:46:40.292 --> 00:46:42.090
So, I can walk
around down the hall,

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:44.757
and if I see a Black
male that has a suit on,

00:46:44.757 --> 00:46:45.840
that's an easy connection.

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:47.370
Hey, how's it going?

00:46:47.370 --> 00:46:48.640
That's an easy connection.

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:51.600
The more difficult
connection is with someone

00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:54.290
that doesn't look like me,
that doesn't act like me.

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:57.300
And if I can find a way
to make that connection--

00:46:57.300 --> 00:46:59.250
or doesn't think the
same way that I do--

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:03.430
then what ultimately happens
is it enhances who I am.

00:47:03.430 --> 00:47:05.483
And I think if you
all, in this project,

00:47:05.483 --> 00:47:06.900
what I would love
to see out of it

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:12.120
is how you went about getting
outside of your own comfort

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:15.450
zone, finding a way to
connect with someone that's

00:47:15.450 --> 00:47:16.680
really different from you.

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:19.410
Because in the
process of doing that,

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:24.474
I think we as MIT could learn
how to actually do that.

00:47:24.474 --> 00:47:26.520
And that's something
that we need to learn.

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:29.170
Not just MIT, but
other places, too.

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:30.810
One of the things that--

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:32.580
and I'll end with this--

00:47:32.580 --> 00:47:35.280
as an undergraduate
student, I worked at NASA.

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:37.410
And my advisor was
Franklin Chang-Diaz.

00:47:37.410 --> 00:47:39.030
He's an astronaut.

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:40.430
And when I graduated--

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:42.430
I think I may have mentioned
this to you before.

00:47:42.430 --> 00:47:45.905
When I graduated, I
was admitted into MIT

00:47:45.905 --> 00:47:48.030
and had to go back to NASA
to work for that summer.

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.350
And at the end of the summer,
I gave a presentation.

00:47:50.350 --> 00:47:53.610
And we talked, and we
were talking about MIT.

00:47:53.610 --> 00:47:56.130
And one of the things that
he said to me that I'll never

00:47:56.130 --> 00:48:00.880
forget was, when you go to
MIT, don't lose your humanity.

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:03.840
I didn't quite understand
what he was talking about.

00:48:03.840 --> 00:48:05.370
But having been
here for a while,

00:48:05.370 --> 00:48:10.620
I see that, in the
midst of our deep drive

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:15.930
and in the midst of our
endless pursuit of excellence,

00:48:15.930 --> 00:48:19.260
it's easy to lose our humanity.

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:21.900
That human connection.

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:24.810
I think we've done
a really good job

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:27.360
in the last decade
of making sure

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:29.430
that students and
faculty and staff

00:48:29.430 --> 00:48:30.990
have that human connection.

00:48:30.990 --> 00:48:33.900
And I would love to
see this project add

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:37.110
to what is being done to
help us really understand

00:48:37.110 --> 00:48:38.610
how to maintain a
human connection,

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:40.970
because that's most important.

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:42.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: And I'll
just add one other thing.

00:48:42.970 --> 00:48:45.730
One of the reasons that
we watched the film

00:48:45.730 --> 00:48:47.530
that we watched, or
the selection from it,

00:48:47.530 --> 00:48:50.590
is that another way that
I could have approached

00:48:50.590 --> 00:48:56.170
making this film would have been
to essentially cast students

00:48:56.170 --> 00:48:59.080
myself and go interview them.

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:02.980
And I was somewhat resistant
to do that because I was afraid

00:49:02.980 --> 00:49:06.880
that it would end up being like
a more contemporary version

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:11.350
of that, which is a top-down
look at the student body

00:49:11.350 --> 00:49:15.290
and what MIT students are
like and what they do.

00:49:15.290 --> 00:49:17.350
And so this is kind
of an experiment,

00:49:17.350 --> 00:49:19.510
and I'm hoping that
you'll participate

00:49:19.510 --> 00:49:25.225
by looking at each other,
looking at yourselves.

00:49:25.225 --> 00:49:26.350
So, we'll see what happens.

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:26.902
But anyway.

00:49:26.902 --> 00:49:29.110
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Do you
all have any major concerns

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:29.860
about the project?

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:36.015
Good.

00:49:36.015 --> 00:49:36.515
Yes?

00:49:36.515 --> 00:49:38.015
AUDIENCE: It's not
really a concern.

00:49:38.015 --> 00:49:39.660
I'm just wondering
because I'm just

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:43.930
speaking-- you were
talking about [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:43.930 --> 00:49:46.180
but at the same
time, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:49:53.900 --> 00:49:58.810
So, after watching this
video, [INAUDIBLE]??

00:50:03.440 --> 00:50:07.290
If you were a woman, you would
be happy [INAUDIBLE] discussion

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:07.790
around that.

00:50:07.790 --> 00:50:13.870
But then, the way we look
at it now, [INAUDIBLE]??

00:50:13.870 --> 00:50:17.780
So now, we're talking about
the issues that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:50:17.780 --> 00:50:21.290
Are we going to touch on
those issues [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:23.810
few years from now, and
then perhaps watching

00:50:23.810 --> 00:50:26.180
the video that was
made from this time,

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:28.115
they will be like,
oh, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:50:28.115 --> 00:50:30.380
My first impression was
like, wow, that's great.

00:50:30.380 --> 00:50:33.800
It's only been 50 years or
60 years and we're here.

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:34.640
That's awesome.

00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:36.650
It really made me happy.

00:50:36.650 --> 00:50:39.740
Would that be reflected
in that video?

00:50:39.740 --> 00:50:43.465
Or is it something that, you
know, it's like [INAUDIBLE]??

00:50:43.465 --> 00:50:44.840
CHRIS BOEBEL: You
mean this video

00:50:44.840 --> 00:50:46.380
that we're talking about now?

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:47.810
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:50:47.810 --> 00:50:49.721
Is there a fine line?

00:50:49.721 --> 00:50:52.076
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:52.076 --> 00:50:53.960
How should we go about that?

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:57.920
CHRIS BOEBEL: I hope
there's not a fine line.

00:50:57.920 --> 00:51:00.860
Certainly there shouldn't be
a fine line in what you do.

00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:04.730
I mean, I can't tell you
that if you find somebody

00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:09.860
who is the poster child
for whatever, IHTFP,

00:51:09.860 --> 00:51:13.160
that it's going
to be no problem.

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:14.930
But the goal, the
whole point of this,

00:51:14.930 --> 00:51:20.570
is to erase that fine
line, or blur it.

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:26.390
Because as I said, the very
simple way to do this project

00:51:26.390 --> 00:51:30.813
would be for me, with
the help of the dean

00:51:30.813 --> 00:51:32.480
of undergraduate
education or something,

00:51:32.480 --> 00:51:36.560
to cast students
and profile them.

00:51:36.560 --> 00:51:39.170
And I was resistant to
doing that because I thought

00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:41.990
that there was a danger
that it could end up

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:44.870
being like a slicker,
contemporary version of what

00:51:44.870 --> 00:51:46.310
we watched.

00:51:46.310 --> 00:51:49.130
So, as I said, this
is an experiment

00:51:49.130 --> 00:51:50.680
to try to do
something different.

00:51:50.680 --> 00:51:51.805
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Could I--

00:51:51.805 --> 00:51:54.228
I'm sorry, it could be very
well because of how noisy

00:51:54.228 --> 00:51:54.770
this room is.

00:51:54.770 --> 00:51:58.830
So, basically,
your question is--

00:51:58.830 --> 00:52:01.710
CHRIS BOEBEL: Are
there boundaries?

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:04.700
CHRISTINE WALLEY: We saw it was
very much a PR film from a very

00:52:04.700 --> 00:52:07.040
top-down point of view.

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:08.660
It felt very contrived.

00:52:08.660 --> 00:52:11.382
And we laughed at a
lot of it because we

00:52:11.382 --> 00:52:12.340
thought it was amusing.

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:13.420
One of the questions
we found, then,

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:16.060
so if you want to get at this
question about MIT students,

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:18.895
how do you do it not in that--

00:52:18.895 --> 00:52:22.390
not in a way that
it's not about PR,

00:52:22.390 --> 00:52:24.777
but it's about trying to
capture something that's more--

00:52:24.777 --> 00:52:26.402
in a way, we've had
so many discussions

00:52:26.402 --> 00:52:28.408
about the real relation
and all this stuff.

00:52:28.408 --> 00:52:29.825
I don't want to
use that language,

00:52:29.825 --> 00:52:32.360
but how do you get
at something that's

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:36.193
far more open and evolving,
and it's not just a PR look?

00:52:36.193 --> 00:52:38.610
So, that's one of the questions
that I have for all of you

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:40.310
then.

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:41.610
I know this exercise--

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:43.550
[INAUDIBLE] Some,
but not that much.

00:52:43.550 --> 00:52:46.262
But in terms of actually going
about and shooting an interview

00:52:46.262 --> 00:52:48.220
with the students, do
you have any ideas on how

00:52:48.220 --> 00:52:50.470
you might go about
shooting or conducting

00:52:50.470 --> 00:52:54.150
an interview in a way that
wouldn't have it be just PR,

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:56.400
but actually
getting [INAUDIBLE]??

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:58.960
And you'd be interested in that?

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:01.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
I mean, this partly

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:03.870
having asking you
folks to participate

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:06.870
is part of my answer,
my attempted answer,

00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:09.840
at that very question
because how do you

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:11.140
get at those kinds of things?

00:53:11.140 --> 00:53:14.250
Well, via MIT students
is the first part.

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:17.810
And the second part
is somewhat up to you.

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:20.570
AUDIENCE: I think that
rather than asking,

00:53:20.570 --> 00:53:25.140
or in addition to asking
people about their experience

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:27.450
at MIT, because then, when
you create an original,

00:53:27.450 --> 00:53:29.310
it's constructed
in a certain way.

00:53:29.310 --> 00:53:31.558
And with a camera,
you tend to emphasize,

00:53:31.558 --> 00:53:33.475
I guess, [INAUDIBLE]
posting those, especially

00:53:33.475 --> 00:53:39.340
the first time that you
are in a short interview.

00:53:39.340 --> 00:53:42.810
So it would be nice to see
people in their own space,

00:53:42.810 --> 00:53:44.130
like walking around the lab.

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:45.360
So they're at cafes.

00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:50.340
They go to the corridors,
the teams they work on.

00:53:50.340 --> 00:53:52.080
Because I think just
walking in space

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:56.310
brings a different kind of
experiential dimension to it.

00:53:56.310 --> 00:53:58.030
And the kind of
stories you tell--

00:53:58.030 --> 00:54:00.030
they're still stories,
they're still narratives,

00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:05.020
but they reflect more your
body experience around MIT.

00:54:05.020 --> 00:54:08.660
So they might tell
much more things

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:11.172
than a [INAUDIBLE]
kind of a storyline.

00:54:11.172 --> 00:54:13.380
So I was thinking about
doing something [INAUDIBLE]..

00:54:13.380 --> 00:54:14.422
CHRIS BOEBEL: Absolutely.

00:54:14.422 --> 00:54:17.992
I mean, if you look at, again,
the film that we looked at, I

00:54:17.992 --> 00:54:19.200
talked about how I couldn't--

00:54:19.200 --> 00:54:21.850
I was really taken by
the fact that it was

00:54:21.850 --> 00:54:23.520
so much about infrastructure.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:24.810
This is Walker Memorial.

00:54:24.810 --> 00:54:26.280
This is the president's house.

00:54:26.280 --> 00:54:29.637
But it's all
outside in, standing

00:54:29.637 --> 00:54:30.720
on the outside looking in.

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:32.553
And what you're talking
about is inside out,

00:54:32.553 --> 00:54:38.130
which is, again, exactly
what I'd love to get.

00:54:38.130 --> 00:54:40.222
CHRISTOPHER JONES: And
I think that gets at--

00:54:40.222 --> 00:54:41.680
which is why I'm
excited about it--

00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:46.170
I think gets at that
issue of humanity.

00:54:46.170 --> 00:54:48.420
Because you all are there.

00:54:48.420 --> 00:54:50.670
You're connecting
with the students.

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.450
And the students are
the lifeblood of MIT.

00:54:52.450 --> 00:54:54.960
So the question is, what
does the lifeblood of MIT

00:54:54.960 --> 00:54:57.160
really look like?

00:54:57.160 --> 00:54:59.960
And I think that's
the real question.

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:04.470
So I'm excited about
seeing what the answer is.

00:55:04.470 --> 00:55:06.590
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
I will say, too,

00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:10.830
so Christopher runs the MSRP
program every summer, which

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:12.960
is a program for
undergrads to come to MIT

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:15.090
and do graduate research.

00:55:15.090 --> 00:55:17.508
And we do video work
with them every summer.

00:55:17.508 --> 00:55:19.800
And one of the things that
he does, which is wonderful,

00:55:19.800 --> 00:55:22.020
is he gives cameras
to their students.

00:55:22.020 --> 00:55:25.080
And these are not people
who are in a film class.

00:55:25.080 --> 00:55:26.880
They're not people who
are trained at all.

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:31.230
But they get amazing stuff
that we could never get.

00:55:31.230 --> 00:55:34.410
So clearly, you're going to
have a different perspective

00:55:34.410 --> 00:55:36.510
just by virtue of who you are.

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:38.663
Plus, you're taking this
class, so hopefully--

00:55:38.663 --> 00:55:40.080
it's only been a
couple of months,

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:41.180
but you've learned
something, right?

00:55:41.180 --> 00:55:42.030
CHRISTOPHER JONES: [CHUCKLES]

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:43.405
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So we've talked

00:55:43.405 --> 00:55:45.723
about lots of different
styles, or genres,

00:55:45.723 --> 00:55:48.662
of documentary filmmaking
and different techniques.

00:55:48.662 --> 00:55:51.120
So you can think about-- so
Katarina, what you were talking

00:55:51.120 --> 00:55:52.530
about is kind of the
direct cinema style, where

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:53.947
you take a camera
and move around,

00:55:53.947 --> 00:55:55.860
and you follow people
through their spaces,

00:55:55.860 --> 00:55:57.878
whether it's labs or
dorms or whatever it is.

00:55:57.878 --> 00:56:00.420
And then we were talking in the
last class about Errol Morris

00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:03.300
and the interview style
where it's not really

00:56:03.300 --> 00:56:04.840
about the interviewers'
questions.

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:06.240
It's about just
allowing somebody

00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:08.615
to get into their own head
space and talk about what they

00:56:08.615 --> 00:56:11.130
think is important to them.

00:56:11.130 --> 00:56:12.960
It could be the focus
where the camera's

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:14.267
focusing on interactions.

00:56:14.267 --> 00:56:16.350
So are there certain kinds
of student interactions

00:56:16.350 --> 00:56:18.000
that you would want to convey?

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.410
So it would be
focusing on a student

00:56:20.410 --> 00:56:23.000
but also, that student who's
very much in interaction

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:23.500
with others.

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:25.680
And if you wanted to focus on
that, what kind of interactions

00:56:25.680 --> 00:56:26.920
would you want to focus on?

00:56:26.920 --> 00:56:29.747
I'm trying to think of
other examples of some

00:56:29.747 --> 00:56:31.580
of the other styles
that we've talked about.

00:56:31.580 --> 00:56:32.705
We've still got more to go.

00:56:32.705 --> 00:56:36.040
We're doing sensory
ethnography in the next class.

00:56:36.040 --> 00:56:38.620
And the sensory ethnography
perspective on documentary

00:56:38.620 --> 00:56:42.880
is really trying to get at the
sensory aspects of people's

00:56:42.880 --> 00:56:44.170
day-to-day worlds.

00:56:44.170 --> 00:56:46.560
So for example, if you
were working in a lab--

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:48.210
and for those of you
who saw the DV Lab

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:51.300
shorts from the last class,
the very first one by Christina

00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:54.240
is a bit in that mode, where
she did this short film

00:56:54.240 --> 00:57:00.840
about herself working in
a lab and the repetitive

00:57:00.840 --> 00:57:02.610
nature of the lab work.

00:57:02.610 --> 00:57:04.590
So she cut it in a
way where it was very

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:05.880
fun and enjoyable to watch.

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:07.463
But it was very much
about, OK, here's

00:57:07.463 --> 00:57:09.845
the nature of what it's
like to work in a lab.

00:57:09.845 --> 00:57:11.220
So is there a way
where you could

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.140
do a sensory
ethnographic perspective

00:57:13.140 --> 00:57:15.960
on doing an interview with an
MIT student, where you would

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:20.570
kind of get at a student's
sensory experience of what it's

00:57:20.570 --> 00:57:22.028
like to be at MIT
or to do whatever

00:57:22.028 --> 00:57:23.987
it is that they're
interested, whatever they're

00:57:23.987 --> 00:57:25.000
passionate about.

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:25.660
I'm trying to think of--

00:57:25.660 --> 00:57:26.630
Chris, can you think
of other techniques?

00:57:26.630 --> 00:57:29.005
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, I mean,
there's the Jean Rouch thing.

00:57:29.005 --> 00:57:33.220
An interview-based film could
be an Errol Morris-style film

00:57:33.220 --> 00:57:35.110
or just a talking head film.

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.360
But think about Jean Rouch
and Chronicle of a Summer.

00:57:37.360 --> 00:57:39.820
Basically, get people together.

00:57:39.820 --> 00:57:42.820
Don't be afraid of being
involved, inserting yourself

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:46.780
in the film, and
get people talking.

00:57:46.780 --> 00:57:49.670
Be intentionally
provocative or not

00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:51.430
and get people
talking about whatever

00:57:51.430 --> 00:57:53.597
it is that you're interested
in so that, in a sense,

00:57:53.597 --> 00:57:55.480
they're almost
interviewing each other.

00:57:55.480 --> 00:57:56.500
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And Jean Rouch, also,

00:57:56.500 --> 00:57:58.000
think about the
technique in Jaguar,

00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:01.102
where he got together with
Damouré and other people that

00:58:01.102 --> 00:58:02.560
he [INAUDIBLE] made
that film with,

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:05.740
and he had them think out
a skit that would improvise

00:58:05.740 --> 00:58:06.495
their lives.

00:58:06.495 --> 00:58:07.870
So it was based
on improvisation,

00:58:07.870 --> 00:58:10.060
of people improvising
and playing

00:58:10.060 --> 00:58:13.730
with things that they wanted to
convey about their own lives.

00:58:13.730 --> 00:58:15.730
I mean, is there a way
we can use that technique

00:58:15.730 --> 00:58:17.212
to get at these questions?

00:58:17.212 --> 00:58:18.688
I'm trying to think
of other ones--

00:58:18.688 --> 00:58:21.640
Jean Rouch, direct cinema,
sensory ethnography,

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:24.185
the Errol Morris
[INAUDIBLE] style.

00:58:24.185 --> 00:58:25.060
CHRIS BOEBEL: Vérité.

00:58:25.060 --> 00:58:26.192
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:26.192 --> 00:58:27.900
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Now,
that's a question.

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:30.354
How would you do a vérité
take on an MIT student?

00:58:30.354 --> 00:58:31.445
[CHUCKLES]

00:58:31.445 --> 00:58:32.820
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Well, it would have

00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:34.605
to be about MIT,
not MIT students.

00:58:34.605 --> 00:58:36.230
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Because I don't know.

00:58:36.230 --> 00:58:40.410
You're at a bit
of a disadvantage,

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:42.050
just in terms of
what we've already

00:58:42.050 --> 00:58:42.840
talked about in our class.

00:58:42.840 --> 00:58:44.300
But we have been trying
to go through all

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:45.740
these different kinds
of documentary styles

00:58:45.740 --> 00:58:47.280
and techniques
for different ways

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:49.970
that students might get at
various kinds of questions

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:50.830
about science and--

00:58:50.830 --> 00:58:53.840
in terms of filming science
and engineering more broadly.

00:58:53.840 --> 00:58:56.922
So the idea is to give lots
of different examples of how

00:58:56.922 --> 00:58:58.630
you could do things
and then let students

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:01.198
take the tools that they
are most excited about

00:59:01.198 --> 00:59:02.990
and do something really
creative with that.

00:59:02.990 --> 00:59:04.032
CHRIS BOEBEL: Cool, cool.

00:59:04.032 --> 00:59:05.450
And I would just say--

00:59:05.450 --> 00:59:08.390
and you already brought this
up, but you may have something

00:59:08.390 --> 00:59:12.240
to say about this, too--
just thinking about who you

00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:16.880
or, if it's a group of people,
what group you actually

00:59:16.880 --> 00:59:21.798
talk to or profile is crucial.

00:59:21.798 --> 00:59:23.090
And there are two things there.

00:59:23.090 --> 00:59:25.850
One is don't just
interview your roommate

00:59:25.850 --> 00:59:28.945
because they're your roommate,
and it's really easy.

00:59:28.945 --> 00:59:31.070
But at the same time, you
also want to have access.

00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:34.130
You want to have that
comfort level, potentially,

00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:35.830
that we've talked about.

00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:37.310
As you've all
experienced now, you

00:59:37.310 --> 00:59:39.590
walk into a room of
strangers with a camera,

00:59:39.590 --> 00:59:41.090
and it's very
different than walking

00:59:41.090 --> 00:59:43.460
into a room of strangers
without a camera.

00:59:43.460 --> 00:59:44.930
So there are two tensions there.

00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:47.150
There's one, filming
what you know,

00:59:47.150 --> 00:59:48.950
filming people that
already know you

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:51.530
and that you have a
rapport with, but two,

00:59:51.530 --> 00:59:55.500
don't just do that
because it's easy.

00:59:55.500 --> 00:59:59.600
So I guess what I'm hoping is
that you'll think about some

00:59:59.600 --> 01:00:02.720
of the issues that
we've talked about today

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:05.840
and come up with a
subject that you have

01:00:05.840 --> 01:00:10.010
a sense will get at
some of those things

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:11.630
in an unfiltered way--

01:00:11.630 --> 01:00:16.670
I'm definitely not interested
in the Potemkin village

01:00:16.670 --> 01:00:19.760
version of this--

01:00:19.760 --> 01:00:24.210
but that also challenges you
as a filmmaker and as a person.

01:00:24.210 --> 01:00:25.627
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
That's also what

01:00:25.627 --> 01:00:26.870
Dean Jones was talking
about in terms of getting

01:00:26.870 --> 01:00:28.400
outside your comfort zone.

01:00:28.400 --> 01:00:29.540
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Yeah.

01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:30.290
I would just add--

01:00:30.290 --> 01:00:33.140
I would just
underscore that and say

01:00:33.140 --> 01:00:39.530
that I would love to see you
all lean more towards the side

01:00:39.530 --> 01:00:42.530
of those that you don't
have relationships--

01:00:42.530 --> 01:00:46.640
I know access is always an issue
with any sort of these things.

01:00:46.640 --> 01:00:51.260
But I think even in the process
of having to gain access

01:00:51.260 --> 01:00:55.790
to some individual or group
that you haven't had access to

01:00:55.790 --> 01:00:59.300
before, there's a lot
that you can learn from.

01:00:59.300 --> 01:01:00.950
There's a deep
learning experience.

01:01:00.950 --> 01:01:04.220
There's a book that was just
released, and you all came in--

01:01:04.220 --> 01:01:06.770
no, we put it up on the web
when she came and spoke.

01:01:06.770 --> 01:01:08.780
It's called The
Warmth of Other Suns.

01:01:08.780 --> 01:01:10.940
And it's about the
Great Migration

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:16.970
of Blacks from the Southern
states to the Northern states.

01:01:16.970 --> 01:01:19.370
And there was an individual,
Isabel Wilkerson,

01:01:19.370 --> 01:01:22.640
who interviewed
over 1,200 people

01:01:22.640 --> 01:01:26.495
in order to understand what
happened in this process.

01:01:26.495 --> 01:01:28.370
It happened from the
beginning of World War I

01:01:28.370 --> 01:01:29.790
until about 1970.

01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:32.120
And over six million
African-Americans

01:01:32.120 --> 01:01:35.450
moved from the
South to the North.

01:01:35.450 --> 01:01:38.120
But in order to get
all these interviews,

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:40.910
she had to go through
so many different hoops

01:01:40.910 --> 01:01:43.820
just to get access to people.

01:01:43.820 --> 01:01:46.145
Because now, there are--

01:01:46.145 --> 01:01:48.020
I don't remember exactly
what they call them,

01:01:48.020 --> 01:01:50.420
but they're groups
or committees that

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:52.160
are in different
places in the North

01:01:52.160 --> 01:01:55.100
that are based on where
people were from in the South.

01:01:55.100 --> 01:01:59.840
So there's a Hattiesburg,
Mississippi club in Chicago.

01:01:59.840 --> 01:02:01.850
And in order for
her to get access

01:02:01.850 --> 01:02:03.690
to the Hattiesburg,
Mississippi club,

01:02:03.690 --> 01:02:05.870
she had to talk to the
people that ran the club.

01:02:05.870 --> 01:02:07.230
They had to have a meeting.

01:02:07.230 --> 01:02:08.780
And they had to have a
vote on whether or not

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:11.113
they were going to give her
access to come into the club

01:02:11.113 --> 01:02:12.622
and interact with them.

01:02:12.622 --> 01:02:14.330
So you may have to go
through some hoops.

01:02:14.330 --> 01:02:16.455
But even in the process of
going through the hoops,

01:02:16.455 --> 01:02:18.878
I think you'll learn
a lot about yourself.

01:02:18.878 --> 01:02:20.670
You'll learn a lot
about other individuals.

01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:22.805
And I think it'll definitely
enrich the project.

01:02:26.800 --> 01:02:29.670
CHRIS BOEBEL: And one last thing
I'll just say about Dean Jones,

01:02:29.670 --> 01:02:31.420
his office is not
sponsoring this project.

01:02:31.420 --> 01:02:32.503
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Oh, no.

01:02:32.503 --> 01:02:34.830
CHRIS BOEBEL: He's just here
because he's interested,

01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:36.390
just to make it clear.

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:39.860
So this is not--

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:44.110
this is as unfiltered as
I can make it, hopefully.

01:02:44.110 --> 01:02:45.390
But anyway, I know--

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:45.530
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Cool, thanks.

01:02:45.530 --> 01:02:47.010
CHRIS BOEBEL: --you've
got a lot going on.

01:02:47.010 --> 01:02:48.052
But thank you for coming.

01:02:48.052 --> 01:02:49.343
CHRISTOPHER JONES: I'm excited.

01:02:49.343 --> 01:02:49.860
Good stuff.

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:52.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: Any
questions for Christopher?

01:02:52.320 --> 01:02:55.200
And we'll talk more
about the nitty-gritty

01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:58.490
of this project later in class.

01:02:58.490 --> 01:02:59.490
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Cool.

01:02:59.490 --> 01:03:00.300
CHRIS BOEBEL: Great,
well, thanks so much.

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:00.930
CHRISTOPHER JONES: All
right, thanks a lot.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:01.930
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK, thanks a lot.

01:03:01.930 --> 01:03:02.805
Don't forget the mic.

01:03:02.805 --> 01:03:04.435
CHRISTOPHER JONES: Cool.

01:03:04.435 --> 01:03:05.310
Nice to meet you all.

01:03:07.597 --> 01:03:08.680
Good luck with everything.

01:03:08.680 --> 01:03:09.930
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, thank you.

01:03:09.930 --> 01:03:10.820
Talk to you soon.

01:03:10.820 --> 01:03:11.440
CHRISTOPHER JONES:
Thank you very much.

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:12.344
Appreciate it.

01:03:12.344 --> 01:03:12.844
Bye-bye.

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:16.380
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK,
well, why don't we

01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:17.975
take just a short break.

01:03:17.975 --> 01:03:19.350
And then when we
come back, we're

01:03:19.350 --> 01:03:23.340
going to dive into some more
specific kinds of interview

01:03:23.340 --> 01:03:25.680
techniques that you
can use just in terms

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:27.850
of thinking about how
to conduct an interview,

01:03:27.850 --> 01:03:30.270
how to set things
up technically.

01:03:30.270 --> 01:03:32.370
And then we can
also try to go over

01:03:32.370 --> 01:03:35.020
some of the specifics
of this project.

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:35.910
Thanks.

01:03:35.910 --> 01:03:37.090
We'll get started here.

01:03:37.090 --> 01:03:41.040
And the first thing I want to do
is just run through some basic

01:03:41.040 --> 01:03:44.850
considerations that you'll want
to be thinking about when you

01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:48.030
tackle an
interview-based project--

01:03:48.030 --> 01:03:51.480
and, again, interview-based
being interpreted

01:03:51.480 --> 01:03:57.030
fairly broadly as anything from
a talking head-based project,

01:03:57.030 --> 01:04:00.030
as, quote unquote, like
an Errol Morris film,

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:02.820
through something that's
a little more interactive,

01:04:02.820 --> 01:04:06.000
something like we saw in
Chronicle of a Summer,

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.110
and anything in between.

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:11.400
So obviously, if you're
going to do anything

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:13.080
like that that's
interview-based,

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:15.040
audio becomes really,
really crucial.

01:04:15.040 --> 01:04:18.270
And if you remember early on,
one of the things that I said

01:04:18.270 --> 01:04:21.000
was that sometimes sound is
more important than picture.

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:25.770
And this is a situation in
which that becomes really true.

01:04:25.770 --> 01:04:29.400
Obviously, if you can't hear
what the interviewee's saying

01:04:29.400 --> 01:04:33.400
or if it's poorly recorded or
somehow, something goes wrong,

01:04:33.400 --> 01:04:37.240
you don't really have
a lot to work with.

01:04:37.240 --> 01:04:40.682
So I don't know-- have all of
you had a chance to play around

01:04:40.682 --> 01:04:43.140
with the lavalier microphone,
or have you been mainly using

01:04:43.140 --> 01:04:44.956
the boom at this point?

01:04:44.956 --> 01:04:46.240
AUDIENCE: Just the boom.

01:04:46.240 --> 01:04:47.100
CHRIS BOEBEL: Just the boom?

01:04:47.100 --> 01:04:48.558
Has anybody messed
with a lavalier?

01:04:48.558 --> 01:04:49.260
You?

01:04:49.260 --> 01:04:50.760
OK.

01:04:50.760 --> 01:04:54.210
So this is a situation
in which you may want

01:04:54.210 --> 01:04:56.850
to consider using the lavalier.

01:04:56.850 --> 01:04:59.400
And remember, again, that
the lavalier microphone

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:03.690
is omnidirectional,
which means that it's

01:05:03.690 --> 01:05:08.310
really great at picking
up sound in any direction

01:05:08.310 --> 01:05:09.760
from where it's located.

01:05:09.760 --> 01:05:14.520
So in a relatively
quiet room, where

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:17.130
you can put it fairly close
to the person's mouth,

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:19.980
it's a great microphone
for an interview.

01:05:19.980 --> 01:05:21.090
I'm wearing one right now.

01:05:21.090 --> 01:05:23.920
Chris is wearing one right now.

01:05:23.920 --> 01:05:25.455
It's very close to me.

01:05:25.455 --> 01:05:26.830
This one, of
course, is wireless,

01:05:26.830 --> 01:05:28.938
which is really great.

01:05:28.938 --> 01:05:30.840
But we don't have wireless.

01:05:30.840 --> 01:05:33.270
So thinking about
and making sure

01:05:33.270 --> 01:05:36.300
that you are comfortable
with a lavalier microphone

01:05:36.300 --> 01:05:38.640
might be something to consider.

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:40.680
Now, again, of course,
if you're doing something

01:05:40.680 --> 01:05:43.080
where the person is
walking around in a space,

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:46.320
walking and talking, as
Katarina was talking about,

01:05:46.320 --> 01:05:49.470
that isn't necessarily going
to work as well, primarily

01:05:49.470 --> 01:05:52.500
because we have
wired microphones,

01:05:52.500 --> 01:05:53.710
although you can try it.

01:05:53.710 --> 01:05:54.627
I've done it myself.

01:05:54.627 --> 01:05:57.210
It just means that the person
is tethered to the camera, which

01:05:57.210 --> 01:05:58.080
can be awkward.

01:05:58.080 --> 01:06:02.910
And again, if you're concerned
about impacting the scene

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:05.100
and making the person
feel uncomfortable,

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:09.150
that is something that would
make anyone feel uncomfortable.

01:06:09.150 --> 01:06:09.650
Go ahead.

01:06:09.650 --> 01:06:11.108
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Maybe at the end,

01:06:11.108 --> 01:06:13.290
if you guys are interested,
maybe we can have,

01:06:13.290 --> 01:06:15.660
if there's time at
the end, have Dave

01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:18.263
or you go over using the shotgun
mics on top of these cameras,

01:06:18.263 --> 01:06:20.680
so exactly how you set them
up so you're ready to do that,

01:06:20.680 --> 01:06:21.960
so if you want to
have better sound

01:06:21.960 --> 01:06:23.730
but you want it just on
the camera without wires.

01:06:23.730 --> 01:06:24.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: Now,
remember, the thing

01:06:24.750 --> 01:06:26.417
about putting the
directional microphone

01:06:26.417 --> 01:06:28.010
on top of the
camera, that actually

01:06:28.010 --> 01:06:30.510
can be a pretty good interview
technique, as long as you can

01:06:30.510 --> 01:06:33.330
stay fairly close to the
person and also as long

01:06:33.330 --> 01:06:35.850
as you can stay
pointed at their mouth.

01:06:35.850 --> 01:06:37.455
Because if they turn
their back on you

01:06:37.455 --> 01:06:38.970
if you're walking
through a space,

01:06:38.970 --> 01:06:41.190
their sound is going to
pretty much disappear.

01:06:41.190 --> 01:06:44.670
Because this is so
directional that it's not

01:06:44.670 --> 01:06:47.010
going to pick up anything
except what's bouncing back

01:06:47.010 --> 01:06:50.070
at the microphone from whatever
surface is in front of them,

01:06:50.070 --> 01:06:50.640
like a wall.

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:53.100
AUDIENCE: How close should
you be to the person?

01:06:53.100 --> 01:06:55.110
CHRIS BOEBEL: Maybe
four or five feet.

01:06:55.110 --> 01:06:57.090
Three feet would be great.

01:06:57.090 --> 01:06:59.190
What is that, about four feet?

01:06:59.190 --> 01:07:03.180
Probably about four feet is
as far as you want to be.

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:05.760
AUDIENCE: So say, technically,
if they're walking,

01:07:05.760 --> 01:07:08.805
it would have to be
either on the side--

01:07:08.805 --> 01:07:11.430
CHRIS BOEBEL: On the side or in
front, walking backwards, yeah.

01:07:11.430 --> 01:07:12.650
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
walking backwards.

01:07:12.650 --> 01:07:14.040
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
that would be great.

01:07:14.040 --> 01:07:16.260
Walking backwards would
be the ideal way to do it.

01:07:16.260 --> 01:07:17.310
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:17.310 --> 01:07:18.840
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
very difficult.

01:07:18.840 --> 01:07:21.390
If you're planning on
following someone behind

01:07:21.390 --> 01:07:24.133
and you want to get good
sound of them talking,

01:07:24.133 --> 01:07:26.550
you're pretty much stuck with
either a lavalier microphone

01:07:26.550 --> 01:07:27.060
or the boom.

01:07:27.060 --> 01:07:29.602
Because of course, the boom can
go over the top of the person

01:07:29.602 --> 01:07:32.243
or to the side.

01:07:32.243 --> 01:07:33.660
So that's one
thing, just thinking

01:07:33.660 --> 01:07:36.420
about how you're going
to mic the people.

01:07:36.420 --> 01:07:39.180
And considering using
the lavalier if it

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:43.200
is, in fact, going to be
kind of a sit-down interview.

01:07:43.200 --> 01:07:45.780
Second thing to think about
is, do you mic yourself?

01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:50.070
Do you want your questions to
be part of what you record?

01:07:50.070 --> 01:07:55.320
And obviously, that is
completely your choice.

01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:56.970
There's a whole
school of filmmaking

01:07:56.970 --> 01:08:01.410
where the interviewee--
interviewer, sorry-- is

01:08:01.410 --> 01:08:06.930
cut out, and the questions
are not part of what's used.

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:10.410
And in that case, you
don't need to mic yourself.

01:08:10.410 --> 01:08:11.920
Obviously, they can hear you.

01:08:11.920 --> 01:08:14.400
There's no reason to
record what you're saying.

01:08:14.400 --> 01:08:15.900
What that means,
though, is that you

01:08:15.900 --> 01:08:18.930
need to get people to
answer in complete thoughts.

01:08:18.930 --> 01:08:20.910
So anyway, so do
you mic yourself?

01:08:20.910 --> 01:08:24.270
If you want to have
your questions be

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:27.300
part of the record, part of
what you're going to use,

01:08:27.300 --> 01:08:29.250
you obviously do
need to mic yourself.

01:08:29.250 --> 01:08:31.410
And you could do that
with the lavalier

01:08:31.410 --> 01:08:33.510
and then use the
shotgun microphone

01:08:33.510 --> 01:08:37.683
to record the person, for
instance, or vice versa.

01:08:37.683 --> 01:08:39.600
You should put yourself
on a separate channel,

01:08:39.600 --> 01:08:45.060
though, so you can put the
interviewee on channel 1

01:08:45.060 --> 01:08:47.010
and you on channel
2, so that way you

01:08:47.010 --> 01:08:50.399
can control them
separately in the edit,

01:08:50.399 --> 01:08:52.830
and you have two inputs, which
we've gone over in class.

01:08:52.830 --> 01:08:56.180
And obviously, we can
review that if you need to.

01:08:56.180 --> 01:08:58.680
But to get back to the idea,
then, if you don't mic yourself

01:08:58.680 --> 01:09:01.439
and you don't want to
include the questions,

01:09:01.439 --> 01:09:03.810
you really need people
to say everything

01:09:03.810 --> 01:09:06.990
that you need said in order
to tell whatever story it

01:09:06.990 --> 01:09:08.050
is that you're telling.

01:09:08.050 --> 01:09:09.720
So again, to go
back to this thing

01:09:09.720 --> 01:09:13.350
that we were about to say with
Chris, if I am interviewing

01:09:13.350 --> 01:09:16.240
Chris and I'm basically
trying to tell her life story

01:09:16.240 --> 01:09:18.799
and I say, when were you
born, and she says 1965--

01:09:18.799 --> 01:09:19.993
CHRISTINE WALLEY: '65.

01:09:19.993 --> 01:09:21.660
CHRIS BOEBEL: --I
can't really use that.

01:09:21.660 --> 01:09:23.850
Because all she said was 1965.

01:09:23.850 --> 01:09:26.910
She has to say, I
was born in 1965.

01:09:26.910 --> 01:09:29.130
AUDIENCE: So you
would ask questions

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:34.109
that imply an answer that's
a full sentence, like tell me

01:09:34.109 --> 01:09:37.500
about your childhood.

01:09:37.500 --> 01:09:39.402
And I should say,
I was born in--

01:09:39.402 --> 01:09:40.319
CHRIS BOEBEL: Exactly.

01:09:40.319 --> 01:09:43.930
And sometimes, I will just
be very upfront with people,

01:09:43.930 --> 01:09:48.175
and I will say, try to
answer in complete thoughts.

01:09:48.175 --> 01:09:50.300
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So before
you start the interview

01:09:50.300 --> 01:09:51.590
to alert them to that.

01:09:51.590 --> 01:09:52.700
But people forget.

01:09:52.700 --> 01:09:55.140
CHRIS BOEBEL: And I'll
explain, I'm not going to be--

01:09:55.140 --> 01:09:56.190
no one's going to
hear my question,

01:09:56.190 --> 01:09:57.560
so can you please answer
in complete thoughts?

01:09:57.560 --> 01:09:59.480
And people are usually
pretty good about that.

01:09:59.480 --> 01:10:01.520
One technique that
people also use,

01:10:01.520 --> 01:10:04.490
which can be a little
dangerous, is they will say,

01:10:04.490 --> 01:10:08.220
can you incorporate the
question in the answer?

01:10:08.220 --> 01:10:12.170
So what happens
then is people say--

01:10:12.170 --> 01:10:14.880
you ask, what are
MIT students like?

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:17.150
And they say, when
someone asks me,

01:10:17.150 --> 01:10:20.263
what are MIT students
like, I usually say--

01:10:20.263 --> 01:10:21.680
and so you can see
the difficulty.

01:10:21.680 --> 01:10:23.263
On the one hand,
it's good because you

01:10:23.263 --> 01:10:25.043
get the context for the answer.

01:10:25.043 --> 01:10:26.960
But on the other hand,
it's kind of formulaic,

01:10:26.960 --> 01:10:28.170
and it's a little weird.

01:10:28.170 --> 01:10:32.730
So to go back to
the 1965 example,

01:10:32.730 --> 01:10:35.970
if Chris said, when someone
asks me, when were you born,

01:10:35.970 --> 01:10:38.690
I usually say 1965.

01:10:38.690 --> 01:10:40.790
So it can get a little weird.

01:10:40.790 --> 01:10:42.090
But it is one way to do it.

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:44.090
And it's kind of reliable
because people get it,

01:10:44.090 --> 01:10:45.572
and they're able to do it.

01:10:45.572 --> 01:10:47.780
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But I
think Katarina was also right

01:10:47.780 --> 01:10:50.280
on in terms of trying to think
of can you ask questions that

01:10:50.280 --> 01:10:54.050
are general enough where,
kind of intuitively,

01:10:54.050 --> 01:10:56.390
that kind of information
would be part of the story

01:10:56.390 --> 01:10:57.110
that people tell.

01:10:57.110 --> 01:10:58.652
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
that's just a basic

01:10:58.652 --> 01:11:03.470
of interviewing, asking
open-ended questions, not

01:11:03.470 --> 01:11:05.450
do you like MIT?

01:11:05.450 --> 01:11:06.260
Do you like MIT?

01:11:06.260 --> 01:11:06.860
Yes.

01:11:06.860 --> 01:11:08.390
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:08.390 --> 01:11:10.220
Or no.

01:11:10.220 --> 01:11:13.880
So thinking about
what kind of questions

01:11:13.880 --> 01:11:17.060
you need to get the kinds of
answers that you actually want.

01:11:17.060 --> 01:11:21.350
And they tend to be
very, very open-ended.

01:11:21.350 --> 01:11:24.560
Tell me about your
experience at MIT.

01:11:24.560 --> 01:11:27.860
And then you can ask follow-ups.

01:11:27.860 --> 01:11:33.380
And the follow-up is the real
art, I think, in interviewing.

01:11:33.380 --> 01:11:36.995
Because you go in with
a list of questions

01:11:36.995 --> 01:11:39.620
that you have in your mind that
you think are really important.

01:11:39.620 --> 01:11:42.800
Maybe you've shared them with
the person you're interviewing.

01:11:42.800 --> 01:11:44.360
Maybe you haven't.

01:11:44.360 --> 01:11:46.970
And there are two schools
of thought on that.

01:11:46.970 --> 01:11:52.640
If you share them, then they
may be much better prepared.

01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:53.940
And that may be a good thing.

01:11:53.940 --> 01:11:56.107
On the other hand, they may
be much better prepared,

01:11:56.107 --> 01:11:57.930
and that may be a bad thing.

01:11:57.930 --> 01:12:00.140
It just depends on
what you're going for.

01:12:00.140 --> 01:12:04.100
If you want somebody to talk
in detail about their research

01:12:04.100 --> 01:12:06.410
and to provide
statistics and all sorts

01:12:06.410 --> 01:12:08.630
of specific
information, you really

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:10.460
want them to be prepared.

01:12:10.460 --> 01:12:12.710
On the other hand, if you
want them to speak candidly

01:12:12.710 --> 01:12:17.980
about their life, you may
not want them to be prepared,

01:12:17.980 --> 01:12:19.730
not that you want to
put them on the spot,

01:12:19.730 --> 01:12:21.147
but you want to
have them thinking

01:12:21.147 --> 01:12:22.730
about it fresh
for the first time

01:12:22.730 --> 01:12:25.022
as they're answering the
question, if that makes sense.

01:12:25.022 --> 01:12:26.980
And of course, that also
depends on the person.

01:12:26.980 --> 01:12:29.450
I mean, some people are really
obsessive about preparation

01:12:29.450 --> 01:12:32.570
and get very nervous and
upset when you try to do that.

01:12:32.570 --> 01:12:36.110
And some people are much
better spontaneously.

01:12:36.110 --> 01:12:38.870
But to do a really
good interview that

01:12:38.870 --> 01:12:44.300
isn't necessarily about specific
information or statistics, part

01:12:44.300 --> 01:12:48.470
of your job as an interviewer is
to create an environment where

01:12:48.470 --> 01:12:52.190
people can be spontaneous,
and they are comfortable.

01:12:52.190 --> 01:12:54.890
And one of those
things may be not-- one

01:12:54.890 --> 01:12:57.680
of the elements of creating that
environment may be not giving

01:12:57.680 --> 01:12:59.000
them questions in advance.

01:12:59.000 --> 01:13:02.360
So there isn't necessarily
a right or a wrong answer

01:13:02.360 --> 01:13:02.880
to that.

01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:06.127
But it really varies
and depends on-- go.

01:13:06.127 --> 01:13:07.460
Were you going to say something?

01:13:07.460 --> 01:13:09.800
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Well, I
just had just a follow-up

01:13:09.800 --> 01:13:12.905
when you were talking about
what kinds of questions to ask.

01:13:12.905 --> 01:13:14.780
So Chris gave a really
good example-- tell me

01:13:14.780 --> 01:13:16.910
about your experience at MIT.

01:13:16.910 --> 01:13:19.537
And the good thing about
that is that it's open-ended.

01:13:19.537 --> 01:13:21.620
But the other thing that's
good about that is it's

01:13:21.620 --> 01:13:22.525
also specific.

01:13:22.525 --> 01:13:23.900
Because one of
the things there's

01:13:23.900 --> 01:13:25.567
a tendency, particularly
for those of us

01:13:25.567 --> 01:13:27.230
trained as academics,
we often tend

01:13:27.230 --> 01:13:31.048
to ask abstract questions,
where if you phrase it instead

01:13:31.048 --> 01:13:32.840
of telling me about
your experience at MIT,

01:13:32.840 --> 01:13:34.490
if it's instead,
well, we're trying

01:13:34.490 --> 01:13:38.240
to capture what might be the
ideal typical MIT student,

01:13:38.240 --> 01:13:39.530
and has there been a--

01:13:39.530 --> 01:13:41.300
in this kind of abstracted way.

01:13:41.300 --> 01:13:43.580
And then you ask an abstracted,
intellectual question.

01:13:43.580 --> 01:13:46.790
Then people are
kind of like, uh,

01:13:46.790 --> 01:13:49.550
how do you want me to respond?

01:13:49.550 --> 01:13:51.530
It's not a good
interviewing technique.

01:13:51.530 --> 01:13:53.978
And particularly people
trained in academics

01:13:53.978 --> 01:13:55.520
have to be really
careful about this.

01:13:55.520 --> 01:13:58.490
But so finding a question
that's both open-ended but also

01:13:58.490 --> 01:14:01.160
very specific and grounded, like
tell me about your experience

01:14:01.160 --> 01:14:02.482
at MIT, that's perfect.

01:14:02.482 --> 01:14:04.190
CHRIS BOEBEL: And the
follow-up question,

01:14:04.190 --> 01:14:07.160
I mentioned that the follow-up
question is really important--

01:14:07.160 --> 01:14:08.360
or questions--

01:14:08.360 --> 01:14:09.177
that's why.

01:14:09.177 --> 01:14:11.510
Because if you say, tell me
about your experience at MIT

01:14:11.510 --> 01:14:13.530
and they start
talking about things,

01:14:13.530 --> 01:14:18.650
then you can take what they're
saying and, spontaneously

01:14:18.650 --> 01:14:21.440
yourself, follow up on the
things that are interesting

01:14:21.440 --> 01:14:25.558
and that start to key into
whatever your approach is.

01:14:25.558 --> 01:14:27.350
And of course, you also
need to be prepared

01:14:27.350 --> 01:14:28.892
to throw your approach
out the window

01:14:28.892 --> 01:14:33.500
if it doesn't seem to
be the right way to go.

01:14:33.500 --> 01:14:35.540
One thing that--

01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:37.760
I might have mentioned this
before, at the beginning,

01:14:37.760 --> 01:14:42.955
in the first class-- but
years ago, I saw an interview

01:14:42.955 --> 01:14:44.330
with Errol Morris
where he talked

01:14:44.330 --> 01:14:45.730
about interview techniques.

01:14:45.730 --> 01:14:47.410
And he said somewhat
provocatively,

01:14:47.410 --> 01:14:51.280
because he's provocative,
and facetiously,

01:14:51.280 --> 01:14:54.640
that the main thing
in doing an interview

01:14:54.640 --> 01:14:58.420
is not to listen to what the
people are saying but to appear

01:14:58.420 --> 01:15:00.130
that you're listening.

01:15:00.130 --> 01:15:03.990
And I think what he
meant by that was that

01:15:03.990 --> 01:15:06.490
if you're listening too much,
if you're listening too hard--

01:15:06.490 --> 01:15:09.070
because obviously,
you are listening--

01:15:09.070 --> 01:15:12.855
there's a real desire
to direct the interview

01:15:12.855 --> 01:15:14.980
along particular channels
that you have planned out

01:15:14.980 --> 01:15:16.250
in advance.

01:15:16.250 --> 01:15:22.600
So let's say you're doing
something about MIT students.

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:29.190
And the whole point
of it is to find out

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:34.210
what kind of cooking people
like, where they eat lunch.

01:15:34.210 --> 01:15:37.393
And so all you're
interested in is finding out

01:15:37.393 --> 01:15:40.060
where people eat lunch, and what
they eat lunch, how they do it,

01:15:40.060 --> 01:15:41.260
how they organize it.

01:15:41.260 --> 01:15:44.920
And people might be giving you
amazing material and amazing

01:15:44.920 --> 01:15:47.650
gems of things on
other subjects,

01:15:47.650 --> 01:15:49.570
but you're continually
thinking in your head,

01:15:49.570 --> 01:15:51.528
you're calculating, yeah,
but what about lunch?

01:15:51.528 --> 01:15:52.362
Tell me about lunch?

01:15:52.362 --> 01:15:53.140
Come on, come on.

01:15:53.140 --> 01:15:55.580
And they're laying
out their life story

01:15:55.580 --> 01:15:57.100
and saying all
this amazing stuff.

01:15:57.100 --> 01:15:59.290
And all you're focused
on with tunnel vision

01:15:59.290 --> 01:16:01.900
is the questions that
you want answered.

01:16:01.900 --> 01:16:04.480
And that is very,
very true, especially

01:16:04.480 --> 01:16:09.250
when you're doing journalistic,
directed interviews.

01:16:09.250 --> 01:16:11.110
There is a real tendency
to want to do that

01:16:11.110 --> 01:16:13.510
and to very much, very
tightly, control and direct

01:16:13.510 --> 01:16:14.290
the interview.

01:16:14.290 --> 01:16:16.510
And what he's saying, I
think, in that comment

01:16:16.510 --> 01:16:18.820
is throw that stuff
out the window

01:16:18.820 --> 01:16:21.790
so that when some
kind of amazing thing

01:16:21.790 --> 01:16:24.550
happens in the interview,
if you're not really

01:16:24.550 --> 01:16:26.980
listening to it, then
it'll just happen,

01:16:26.980 --> 01:16:30.640
and you won't try to force
it or jam it into the subject

01:16:30.640 --> 01:16:31.965
that you had preordained.

01:16:31.965 --> 01:16:34.090
CHRISTINE WALLEY: The
slightly less extreme version

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:36.550
of that would be an
anthro take on this,

01:16:36.550 --> 01:16:40.040
where, as opposed to the,
OK, here's my questions

01:16:40.040 --> 01:16:41.080
they haven't answered--

01:16:41.080 --> 01:16:42.910
I'm only gotten to one, and they
haven't answered three, four,

01:16:42.910 --> 01:16:44.830
and five, and, as Chris
is saying, you really

01:16:44.830 --> 01:16:48.040
try to direct them-- but
for anthropologists who

01:16:48.040 --> 01:16:51.802
do ethnographic field work, what
we get trained to do is that,

01:16:51.802 --> 01:16:53.260
again, what's
interesting is what's

01:16:53.260 --> 01:16:55.840
interesting to your
subjects, and so following

01:16:55.840 --> 01:16:56.930
where they're going.

01:16:56.930 --> 01:16:59.410
So in that case, it's actually
listening very intently

01:16:59.410 --> 01:17:02.170
but listening with the
intent not because you

01:17:02.170 --> 01:17:03.670
want to direct them
in a certain way

01:17:03.670 --> 01:17:05.295
but listening with
the intent of trying

01:17:05.295 --> 01:17:08.740
to understand, well, what is
it that's interesting to them?

01:17:08.740 --> 01:17:10.570
And how can I
potentially ask questions

01:17:10.570 --> 01:17:13.660
to help push it along in
that direction of what's

01:17:13.660 --> 01:17:14.830
interesting to them?

01:17:14.830 --> 01:17:19.120
So you're constantly--
again, it's not about,

01:17:19.120 --> 01:17:21.778
as Chris is saying, it's
not about your preordained

01:17:21.778 --> 01:17:24.070
conceptions when you're coming
in about what you should

01:17:24.070 --> 01:17:26.140
be asking but what's
important to this person,

01:17:26.140 --> 01:17:31.870
and how can I help unearth
what it is that they

01:17:31.870 --> 01:17:33.070
find most interesting?

01:17:33.070 --> 01:17:35.830
So it's questions towards
a different kind of end.

01:17:35.830 --> 01:17:39.280
And it's always about being
in response and interacting

01:17:39.280 --> 01:17:42.460
with what people themselves
feel to be important.

01:17:42.460 --> 01:17:45.685
CHRIS BOEBEL: And the other
thing, I think, that is true,

01:17:45.685 --> 01:17:47.560
whether you look at it
as don't listen at all

01:17:47.560 --> 01:17:51.670
or listen very intently,
is that when you're really

01:17:51.670 --> 01:17:55.150
on an agenda as an
interviewer, you

01:17:55.150 --> 01:17:58.485
have a tendency to want to
knock things off the list.

01:17:58.485 --> 01:18:00.610
So if the first question
is, tell me about your MIT

01:18:00.610 --> 01:18:02.455
experience, they talk.

01:18:02.455 --> 01:18:05.410
And you're, OK, next one is
about, where do you have lunch?

01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:07.810
And you're, OK, OK, I gotta
get to the next question.

01:18:07.810 --> 01:18:11.170
You have a tendency
to jump in as it

01:18:11.170 --> 01:18:13.690
seems that they're finishing
with the next question.

01:18:13.690 --> 01:18:15.070
But what I've found--

01:18:15.070 --> 01:18:18.910
and I've done over
a hundred interviews

01:18:18.910 --> 01:18:21.640
this year, so I do this a lot--

01:18:21.640 --> 01:18:25.180
people have a tendency-- there's
kind of a rhythm of speech

01:18:25.180 --> 01:18:28.240
where they finish and
then, if you wait a moment,

01:18:28.240 --> 01:18:29.368
they'll continue.

01:18:29.368 --> 01:18:30.910
And usually, when
they continue, it's

01:18:30.910 --> 01:18:36.370
the deeper, kind of more
personal, more unexpected

01:18:36.370 --> 01:18:37.120
answer.

01:18:37.120 --> 01:18:38.920
Because there's
something about it.

01:18:38.920 --> 01:18:41.380
There's a rhythm
of conversation.

01:18:41.380 --> 01:18:42.065
I say something.

01:18:42.065 --> 01:18:42.940
Chris says something.

01:18:42.940 --> 01:18:43.607
I say something.

01:18:43.607 --> 01:18:44.500
Chris says something.

01:18:44.500 --> 01:18:47.380
Well, if that rhythm is
broken, and suddenly, it's

01:18:47.380 --> 01:18:49.600
more on the person
who's speaking,

01:18:49.600 --> 01:18:52.580
that can be the moment when
something else comes out.

01:18:52.580 --> 01:18:55.182
So there's a
technical reason not

01:18:55.182 --> 01:18:56.890
to jump, as well,
which is that you don't

01:18:56.890 --> 01:18:58.270
want to overlap the audio.

01:18:58.270 --> 01:19:00.040
You don't want their
audio and yours

01:19:00.040 --> 01:19:03.310
to overlap because that can
cause a problem in editing,

01:19:03.310 --> 01:19:06.430
particularly if you're trying
to cut your questions out.

01:19:06.430 --> 01:19:09.640
But also, waiting a beat
just to see if they have

01:19:09.640 --> 01:19:12.430
something else to say, is
often giving them permission

01:19:12.430 --> 01:19:14.360
to say something else.

01:19:14.360 --> 01:19:17.920
And that can be some of the
most interesting and unexpected

01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:20.260
stuff.

01:19:20.260 --> 01:19:25.390
So again, not really trying to
forcibly direct the interview--

01:19:25.390 --> 01:19:28.290
although, of course,
you are directing it--

01:19:28.290 --> 01:19:31.090
is really important.

01:19:31.090 --> 01:19:32.740
Just a couple of other things.

01:19:32.740 --> 01:19:36.100
So we were talking--

01:19:36.100 --> 01:19:39.460
I think I said that,
probably, job number

01:19:39.460 --> 01:19:42.670
one as an interviewer is to
make people feel comfortable

01:19:42.670 --> 01:19:47.280
and to make them feel like
they have permission to speak

01:19:47.280 --> 01:19:54.390
and to say things that
are natural and possibly

01:19:54.390 --> 01:19:56.700
unexpected.

01:19:56.700 --> 01:19:59.160
One really good
way of doing that

01:19:59.160 --> 01:20:01.950
is thinking very
carefully about how you're

01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:03.918
going to start the interview.

01:20:03.918 --> 01:20:06.210
Especially for people who
aren't comfortable with being

01:20:06.210 --> 01:20:08.580
on camera, aren't used
to being on camera,

01:20:08.580 --> 01:20:12.120
there's a real tendency,
particularly when

01:20:12.120 --> 01:20:13.710
you're working on
a bigger production

01:20:13.710 --> 01:20:16.270
with a lot of equipment,
that everything

01:20:16.270 --> 01:20:18.040
gets focused on the equipment.

01:20:18.040 --> 01:20:19.908
So the camera gets set up.

01:20:19.908 --> 01:20:21.450
If you have lights,
you're setting up

01:20:21.450 --> 01:20:22.765
the lights, the audio.

01:20:22.765 --> 01:20:25.390
The poor person who you're going
to interview is sitting there,

01:20:25.390 --> 01:20:27.300
and they're a guinea
pig for the lights,

01:20:27.300 --> 01:20:28.950
and you're adjusting everything.

01:20:28.950 --> 01:20:30.510
Maybe you're slapping
makeup on them.

01:20:30.510 --> 01:20:32.010
You're putting a
microphone on them.

01:20:32.010 --> 01:20:33.635
You're asking them
to do an audio test,

01:20:33.635 --> 01:20:34.802
you know-- what's the level?

01:20:34.802 --> 01:20:35.672
Like, 1, 2, 1, 2.

01:20:35.672 --> 01:20:36.630
Can you say that again?

01:20:36.630 --> 01:20:39.780
Speak a little louder.

01:20:39.780 --> 01:20:41.880
And they get very
uncomfortable, of course,

01:20:41.880 --> 01:20:45.040
because it's this big deal.

01:20:45.040 --> 01:20:47.820
But if you can do that more
unobtrusively-- and obviously,

01:20:47.820 --> 01:20:50.070
one of the nice things is
we hardly have any equipment

01:20:50.070 --> 01:20:55.020
in this class compared
to the load like that--

01:20:55.020 --> 01:20:58.140
so doing it unobtrusively
is very, very helpful

01:20:58.140 --> 01:21:02.250
to setting the tone for
a more natural interview.

01:21:02.250 --> 01:21:05.670
And then the other thing
is how you start it.

01:21:05.670 --> 01:21:09.090
If all of that complicated
setup and running

01:21:09.090 --> 01:21:11.098
around leads up to,
OK, are we ready?

01:21:11.098 --> 01:21:11.640
Are we ready?

01:21:11.640 --> 01:21:12.348
Everyone rolling?

01:21:12.348 --> 01:21:13.740
All right, begin.

01:21:13.740 --> 01:21:14.610
Be natural.

01:21:14.610 --> 01:21:16.770
Go, now.

01:21:16.770 --> 01:21:19.805
That is obviously going to put
a lot of people on the spot

01:21:19.805 --> 01:21:21.180
and make them very
uncomfortable.

01:21:21.180 --> 01:21:22.330
On the other hand--

01:21:22.330 --> 01:21:24.330
and this is another reason
for working in teams,

01:21:24.330 --> 01:21:26.280
even if you don't
have a lot of stuff--

01:21:26.280 --> 01:21:29.160
if one person is doing
all of the technical setup

01:21:29.160 --> 01:21:31.558
and framing the shot and
setting up the camera

01:21:31.558 --> 01:21:33.600
and working with audio
and the other person who's

01:21:33.600 --> 01:21:36.058
going to be doing the interview
is chatting with the person

01:21:36.058 --> 01:21:39.480
the whole time, and then if the
camera just naturally starts

01:21:39.480 --> 01:21:44.160
rolling and you continue your
conversation, then suddenly,

01:21:44.160 --> 01:21:48.660
it's become an interview
without any kind of go.

01:21:48.660 --> 01:21:50.280
Now you're on.

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:51.390
Now we started.

01:21:51.390 --> 01:21:53.400
And that can be
really, really helpful,

01:21:53.400 --> 01:21:56.220
as I said, especially for
people that are not really used

01:21:56.220 --> 01:21:58.110
to being on camera.

01:21:58.110 --> 01:22:01.540
Because you think about it.

01:22:01.540 --> 01:22:04.230
We've had the experience of
being filmed in this class

01:22:04.230 --> 01:22:06.060
off and on over the
last couple of months.

01:22:06.060 --> 01:22:11.070
And how different is it
when the cameras aren't here

01:22:11.070 --> 01:22:14.310
or when we forget that
the cameras are here?

01:22:14.310 --> 01:22:17.700
So the point is that you want to
erase the camera from the room

01:22:17.700 --> 01:22:18.920
as much as possible.

01:22:18.920 --> 01:22:21.420
And the way to do that, as Chris
has said a couple of times,

01:22:21.420 --> 01:22:24.600
have a human subject for
them to focus their attention

01:22:24.600 --> 01:22:27.210
on while the technical
stuff is happening.

01:22:27.210 --> 01:22:31.947
And that isn't so maximized.

01:22:31.947 --> 01:22:34.530
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Just-- unless
your intent is to do something

01:22:34.530 --> 01:22:38.375
Jean Rouchian, where the
idea, then, in that case--

01:22:38.375 --> 01:22:39.750
because that could
be another way

01:22:39.750 --> 01:22:40.860
to do it, then,
where you want it

01:22:40.860 --> 01:22:42.360
to be about the interaction
with the camera,

01:22:42.360 --> 01:22:45.090
but it's meant to be a dialogue,
a discussion back and forth.

01:22:45.090 --> 01:22:45.960
And then you've got
a whole other way

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:47.520
of thinking about the interview.

01:22:47.520 --> 01:22:50.820
But that could be another way
to do it, where it's like, OK,

01:22:50.820 --> 01:22:54.480
it's a conversation between
the camera and this person.

01:22:54.480 --> 01:22:56.220
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yep.

01:22:56.220 --> 01:23:00.940
So just want to throw out a few
video considerations, as well.

01:23:00.940 --> 01:23:05.340
We've been talking a lot about
audio and interview techniques.

01:23:05.340 --> 01:23:10.680
Again, remember that doing an
interview-based film, that's

01:23:10.680 --> 01:23:12.150
very broadly conceived.

01:23:14.850 --> 01:23:18.132
Something that is Errol
Morris-like, or what

01:23:18.132 --> 01:23:20.340
we think of as a conventional
talking head interview,

01:23:20.340 --> 01:23:21.750
is one option.

01:23:21.750 --> 01:23:25.020
And that generally involves
having a camera on a tripod

01:23:25.020 --> 01:23:29.250
and having the person
either looking into the lens

01:23:29.250 --> 01:23:32.550
or at an interviewer
sitting next to the camera,

01:23:32.550 --> 01:23:35.760
just off access from the lens.

01:23:35.760 --> 01:23:39.420
And that's a perfectly
great way to do things.

01:23:39.420 --> 01:23:41.100
But it's not the only
way to do things.

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:43.350
You talked about having
people walking through a space

01:23:43.350 --> 01:23:48.870
and talking, some kind of
environmental interview,

01:23:48.870 --> 01:23:51.510
a hand-held interview where the
camera is actually mobile, even

01:23:51.510 --> 01:23:52.800
if the person's not mobile.

01:23:52.800 --> 01:23:54.360
All of those are other options.

01:23:54.360 --> 01:23:56.310
And they're all
perfectly valid choices.

01:23:56.310 --> 01:23:58.020
You just need to
think about what

01:23:58.020 --> 01:24:02.700
is the option that
communicates what

01:24:02.700 --> 01:24:05.220
you're hoping to communicate?

01:24:05.220 --> 01:24:07.410
Is this a controlled setting?

01:24:07.410 --> 01:24:08.940
Is it an uncontrolled setting?

01:24:08.940 --> 01:24:10.260
Do you want some fluidity?

01:24:10.260 --> 01:24:12.210
Do you want them
to be interacting

01:24:12.210 --> 01:24:13.260
with their environment?

01:24:13.260 --> 01:24:15.270
Or are you going to
get the interaction

01:24:15.270 --> 01:24:16.710
with the environment separately?

01:24:16.710 --> 01:24:19.230
Are you going to interview
them in a more controlled space

01:24:19.230 --> 01:24:21.750
and then film them
doing the stuff

01:24:21.750 --> 01:24:23.200
that they're talking about?

01:24:23.200 --> 01:24:26.710
And I'll talk about
that in a minute.

01:24:26.710 --> 01:24:29.370
So that's one thing, just
thinking about the techniques

01:24:29.370 --> 01:24:31.920
that you're going to
use in the interview

01:24:31.920 --> 01:24:38.520
to convey a feeling
that you want to convey.

01:24:38.520 --> 01:24:42.270
If you are doing something
that is a tripod-based, kind

01:24:42.270 --> 01:24:49.080
of standard, video
interview, you

01:24:49.080 --> 01:24:51.030
want to think about the
framing of the people.

01:24:51.030 --> 01:24:52.980
We talked about framing,
if you remember,

01:24:52.980 --> 01:24:59.190
this idea that the really
powerful parts of the frame

01:24:59.190 --> 01:25:06.420
are the corners, where the
frame is divided into thirds.

01:25:11.520 --> 01:25:13.170
Did a bad job there.

01:25:13.170 --> 01:25:17.100
But these are very
powerful parts of the frame

01:25:17.100 --> 01:25:20.230
because our eyes tend to
be drawn to those points.

01:25:20.230 --> 01:25:24.375
So the standard way
of framing someone--

01:25:24.375 --> 01:25:27.000
and when I say standard, I mean
that sounds kind of pejorative,

01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:29.667
like, oh, well, we don't want to
be standard-- but this actually

01:25:29.667 --> 01:25:32.540
it works really well.

01:25:32.540 --> 01:25:36.540
Framing somebody looking this
way on this side of the frame

01:25:36.540 --> 01:25:40.380
so that their eye is more
or less in that point

01:25:40.380 --> 01:25:45.730
or, alternatively, framing
somebody looking that way,

01:25:45.730 --> 01:25:49.140
so their eye, one of their
eyes, is at that point.

01:25:49.140 --> 01:25:54.450
And that's just a really
good, appealing way

01:25:54.450 --> 01:25:56.598
of framing people.

01:25:56.598 --> 01:25:57.765
And then remembering, also--

01:26:01.400 --> 01:26:02.910
does this go down?--

01:26:05.940 --> 01:26:13.810
if you have somebody
framed like that,

01:26:13.810 --> 01:26:17.620
what's the relationship
between the person's face

01:26:17.620 --> 01:26:19.900
and the background?

01:26:19.900 --> 01:26:22.840
The last thing you want
is for the person's face

01:26:22.840 --> 01:26:24.250
to blend into the background.

01:26:24.250 --> 01:26:27.130
And there are a number of
ways that you can avoid that.

01:26:27.130 --> 01:26:30.400
There's lighting, if you
have light, obviously.

01:26:30.400 --> 01:26:33.130
You don't want the
background to be

01:26:33.130 --> 01:26:37.810
much brighter than the
person's face, for example.

01:26:37.810 --> 01:26:43.460
What you really want is for the
background to fade in some way

01:26:43.460 --> 01:26:45.220
so that the face
kind of jumps out.

01:26:45.220 --> 01:26:47.920
And that's why, for instance,
we've talked about long lenses

01:26:47.920 --> 01:26:52.820
being very good, appealing
ways of shooting people,

01:26:52.820 --> 01:26:54.580
particularly in
interview situations.

01:26:54.580 --> 01:26:58.067
What that does is it allows
you to have the face be

01:26:58.067 --> 01:26:59.650
in very sharp focus
and the background

01:26:59.650 --> 01:27:01.330
to be soft and out of focus.

01:27:01.330 --> 01:27:05.170
And that, again, is a way
of having the face jump out.

01:27:05.170 --> 01:27:08.800
So focus, of course,
is critical--

01:27:08.800 --> 01:27:10.570
always critical but
particularly critical

01:27:10.570 --> 01:27:12.220
when you're shooting people.

01:27:12.220 --> 01:27:15.790
What you want to do
is zoom in all the way

01:27:15.790 --> 01:27:18.490
to the person's face-- to their
eye because you always want

01:27:18.490 --> 01:27:19.960
to focus to the eye--

01:27:19.960 --> 01:27:21.490
focus very carefully.

01:27:21.490 --> 01:27:26.690
And then you can zoom out
to whatever frame you want.

01:27:26.690 --> 01:27:28.490
Also, thinking about
varying the frame.

01:27:28.490 --> 01:27:30.670
This is kind of a
medium-close shot.

01:27:30.670 --> 01:27:36.430
But you could just
as easily film people

01:27:36.430 --> 01:27:40.900
in a wider shot,
a very wide shot.

01:27:43.870 --> 01:27:52.680
Or you could do the Frontline,
60 Minutes thing and film

01:27:52.680 --> 01:27:55.470
people in a very close shot.

01:27:59.473 --> 01:28:00.640
Or you could do all of them.

01:28:00.640 --> 01:28:04.577
You could move the camera
or zoom in and out.

01:28:04.577 --> 01:28:05.410
What were you going?

01:28:05.410 --> 01:28:06.993
AUDIENCE: I have a
technical question.

01:28:06.993 --> 01:28:09.148
So like in Gates of
Heaven, when Errol Morris

01:28:09.148 --> 01:28:09.940
has those amazing--

01:28:09.940 --> 01:28:11.320
I know you weren't
rewatching it,

01:28:11.320 --> 01:28:12.520
but you've seen it
a zillion times--

01:28:12.520 --> 01:28:14.562
but when he does those
amazing portraits and then

01:28:14.562 --> 01:28:16.630
in the background
you have the cacti

01:28:16.630 --> 01:28:20.110
or whatever it is, but the
faces are totally clear,

01:28:20.110 --> 01:28:23.270
but the background
is also totally--

01:28:23.270 --> 01:28:23.770
pops.

01:28:23.770 --> 01:28:25.500
So how exactly did he
shoot that in order

01:28:25.500 --> 01:28:26.500
to get that [INAUDIBLE]?

01:28:26.500 --> 01:28:28.000
CHRIS BOEBEL: Because that's--

01:28:28.000 --> 01:28:32.200
again, he's not doing a
lot of the standard things

01:28:32.200 --> 01:28:33.815
in that film.

01:28:33.815 --> 01:28:35.690
So yeah, the backgrounds
aren't out of focus.

01:28:35.690 --> 01:28:37.930
What he's doing is
carefully orchestrating

01:28:37.930 --> 01:28:40.870
what is in the background so
that it's completely vivid

01:28:40.870 --> 01:28:44.530
but also not completely
fighting the face.

01:28:44.530 --> 01:28:47.860
So you can do it with
art direction, as well.

01:28:47.860 --> 01:28:50.980
There is something back
here that is really vivid

01:28:50.980 --> 01:28:55.803
and kind of dynamic, but
it's not competing-- just

01:28:55.803 --> 01:28:57.220
by the nature of
the framing, it's

01:28:57.220 --> 01:29:01.030
not competing--
with the foreground.

01:29:01.030 --> 01:29:05.500
So art direction is
another way to do this.

01:29:05.500 --> 01:29:09.370
One thing to just note
about art direction--

01:29:09.370 --> 01:29:11.680
this is just a personal
thing of mine--

01:29:11.680 --> 01:29:13.990
there is such a thing
as being too on the nose

01:29:13.990 --> 01:29:15.070
with art direction.

01:29:15.070 --> 01:29:19.630
And what I mean is
let's say you're

01:29:19.630 --> 01:29:26.380
doing something on lab safety.

01:29:26.380 --> 01:29:29.440
And you interview
somebody, and there's

01:29:29.440 --> 01:29:35.620
a sign in the background
that says, like, radiation.

01:29:35.620 --> 01:29:38.020
And it's completely
sharp and in focus.

01:29:38.020 --> 01:29:42.130
And this person is
talking about radiation.

01:29:42.130 --> 01:29:44.780
That's kind of on the nose.

01:29:44.780 --> 01:29:48.520
Now, it might work if this
is a radiation symbol,

01:29:48.520 --> 01:29:52.210
and it's completely soft
and not distracting.

01:29:52.210 --> 01:29:58.450
But there's something about text
and establishing your subjects

01:29:58.450 --> 01:30:00.580
in a very, very
obvious and overt way

01:30:00.580 --> 01:30:04.870
that can get a
little weird for me.

01:30:04.870 --> 01:30:07.120
And there are also
things in the background

01:30:07.120 --> 01:30:10.480
that can be distracting,
unintentionally distracting.

01:30:10.480 --> 01:30:13.300
That's always something
to consider, as well.

01:30:13.300 --> 01:30:15.490
The classic is--

01:30:15.490 --> 01:30:17.778
I don't know if
you saw this, but I

01:30:17.778 --> 01:30:19.570
think it was last year
or the year before--

01:30:19.570 --> 01:30:21.520
Sarah Palin gave an
interview at Thanksgiving

01:30:21.520 --> 01:30:23.103
when she was still
governor of Alaska.

01:30:23.103 --> 01:30:25.300
And they were slaughtering
turkeys behind her.

01:30:25.300 --> 01:30:27.580
And there's this completely
sharp-focused shot

01:30:27.580 --> 01:30:30.700
of this guy slaughtering
turkeys and staring at her.

01:30:30.700 --> 01:30:32.950
And she's talking about
Thanksgiving and the beauty

01:30:32.950 --> 01:30:37.370
of the holiday and so on.

01:30:37.370 --> 01:30:38.530
And it was just hilarious.

01:30:38.530 --> 01:30:40.150
And it was hilarious
because there

01:30:40.150 --> 01:30:44.680
was this utterly distracting
and bizarre event happening

01:30:44.680 --> 01:30:49.750
behind her that the cameraperson
didn't really think about.

01:30:49.750 --> 01:30:51.000
Or you can do that for comic--

01:30:51.000 --> 01:30:52.590
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Or did
and did it intentionally.

01:30:52.590 --> 01:30:53.680
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
yeah, good question.

01:30:53.680 --> 01:30:54.222
I don't know.

01:30:54.222 --> 01:30:56.620
But and obviously, you
can do that intentionally.

01:30:56.620 --> 01:31:00.490
You can have something going on
in the background that comments

01:31:00.490 --> 01:31:03.130
on the foreground intentionally,
either comically--

01:31:03.130 --> 01:31:05.860
it often ends up being comic--

01:31:05.860 --> 01:31:07.913
or not.

01:31:07.913 --> 01:31:09.580
So anyway, these are
all things to think

01:31:09.580 --> 01:31:13.900
about when you're setting up a,
quote unquote, "talking head"

01:31:13.900 --> 01:31:14.830
interview.

01:31:14.830 --> 01:31:17.170
The other thing, which
we've talked a little bit

01:31:17.170 --> 01:31:19.900
about in class, are
lighting techniques.

01:31:19.900 --> 01:31:21.433
Obviously, we don't have lights.

01:31:21.433 --> 01:31:23.350
But that doesn't mean
that you can't use light

01:31:23.350 --> 01:31:28.780
or shouldn't use
light to do better

01:31:28.780 --> 01:31:31.600
jobs with your interviews.

01:31:31.600 --> 01:31:36.220
Well, Max can tell you you can
find lights that can be used.

01:31:36.220 --> 01:31:39.640
He did that a couple of times,
basically using desk lights,

01:31:39.640 --> 01:31:41.800
clip lights, things like that.

01:31:41.800 --> 01:31:42.933
And that's one option.

01:31:42.933 --> 01:31:44.350
The big thing to
think about there

01:31:44.350 --> 01:31:46.990
is not making things
too harsh, not

01:31:46.990 --> 01:31:49.960
making your shadows really
harsh or creating weird lighting

01:31:49.960 --> 01:31:53.560
effects on the people, unless
you're doing it intentionally.

01:31:53.560 --> 01:31:57.430
But if you're doing natural
light, thinking about--

01:31:57.430 --> 01:32:00.100
we've talked about it before
but just to reiterate--

01:32:00.100 --> 01:32:02.590
thinking about where
the light sources are

01:32:02.590 --> 01:32:04.690
and how that affects the
person you're filming.

01:32:04.690 --> 01:32:08.650
So again, in this room, if I was
getting interviewed in the room

01:32:08.650 --> 01:32:12.190
by Andrew, which I am,
if I stood in front

01:32:12.190 --> 01:32:14.860
of the window like
this, especially

01:32:14.860 --> 01:32:17.680
if the blinds were up,
I suddenly get backlit.

01:32:17.680 --> 01:32:21.040
And suddenly, my face is not
bright in the frame at all

01:32:21.040 --> 01:32:23.960
compared to the background.

01:32:23.960 --> 01:32:28.150
But if I come over
here and I stand under,

01:32:28.150 --> 01:32:30.250
or I'm positioned
under, this light so

01:32:30.250 --> 01:32:33.640
that suddenly, I'm in
a pretty good spot,

01:32:33.640 --> 01:32:40.240
my face is brighter than the
background, which is the board.

01:32:40.240 --> 01:32:42.580
So you can position
people in various ways

01:32:42.580 --> 01:32:43.990
to take advantage of that.

01:32:43.990 --> 01:32:48.280
And harsh lighting makes
people look one way.

01:32:48.280 --> 01:32:51.250
Softer lighting-- one
of the nice things

01:32:51.250 --> 01:32:53.530
about this kind of material
is that it breaks up light

01:32:53.530 --> 01:32:54.260
really nicely.

01:32:54.260 --> 01:32:56.140
So this is a very
nice, soft light that

01:32:56.140 --> 01:32:57.440
makes people look really nice.

01:32:57.440 --> 01:32:59.440
So one thing you could
do, which we can't really

01:32:59.440 --> 01:33:01.300
demonstrate with
this camera, is I

01:33:01.300 --> 01:33:04.780
could be interviewed this
way with window light on me.

01:33:04.780 --> 01:33:07.250
And the camera could
be facing here.

01:33:07.250 --> 01:33:11.650
So now I have this really nice,
soft light source lighting me.

01:33:11.650 --> 01:33:14.260
And it would actually be a
really, potentially, very

01:33:14.260 --> 01:33:16.750
nice way of doing it.

01:33:16.750 --> 01:33:18.550
So don't be afraid
to position people

01:33:18.550 --> 01:33:21.760
and to use the
environment around you

01:33:21.760 --> 01:33:23.980
to get what you want.

01:33:23.980 --> 01:33:26.200
And of course, being
outside, the same thing.

01:33:26.200 --> 01:33:29.500
You could film
somebody outside at

01:33:29.500 --> 01:33:31.930
sunset with the sun low
in the sky behind them,

01:33:31.930 --> 01:33:34.540
and suddenly
they're silhouetted.

01:33:34.540 --> 01:33:36.400
Or you could turn them
around, and then you

01:33:36.400 --> 01:33:39.760
have this sort of
golden light on them.

01:33:39.760 --> 01:33:43.210
And those are choices
that you can make.

01:33:43.210 --> 01:33:48.830
So anyway, thoughts or questions
about this at this point?

01:33:51.890 --> 01:33:53.570
OK.

01:33:53.570 --> 01:33:57.980
One other thing that I
wanted to do and then maybe,

01:33:57.980 --> 01:34:01.250
in the last 15 or 20
minutes, we can just

01:34:01.250 --> 01:34:04.370
go over if there are just
basic technical questions

01:34:04.370 --> 01:34:08.900
about the camera or sound
that people want to reiterate.

01:34:08.900 --> 01:34:14.780
I wanted to revisit
this idea of I

01:34:14.780 --> 01:34:20.870
guess is the word illustrating
interviews, the question of,

01:34:20.870 --> 01:34:22.430
OK, so you've done an interview.

01:34:22.430 --> 01:34:24.230
How do you actually
make it come alive?

01:34:24.230 --> 01:34:29.150
And one way to do it, of
course, is to do something,

01:34:29.150 --> 01:34:31.580
as Katarina was talking about.

01:34:31.580 --> 01:34:33.980
Just interview somebody in situ.

01:34:33.980 --> 01:34:38.120
Have them walking around,
describing what they do.

01:34:38.120 --> 01:34:40.943
And have it be very
fluid and very mobile.

01:34:40.943 --> 01:34:42.360
The other way to
do it, of course,

01:34:42.360 --> 01:34:46.250
is to interview them
and then go out and do

01:34:46.250 --> 01:34:48.350
what Errol Morris does--

01:34:48.350 --> 01:34:54.200
get material that reinforces
or reiterates or explicates

01:34:54.200 --> 01:34:55.560
what they're talking about.

01:34:55.560 --> 01:34:59.090
So if somebody is talking about
the biolab that they work in,

01:34:59.090 --> 01:35:02.540
you do a half-hour interview
with them about the biolab.

01:35:02.540 --> 01:35:04.520
And then you go to
the lab, and you

01:35:04.520 --> 01:35:07.940
shoot them working, doing the
things that they were talking

01:35:07.940 --> 01:35:11.120
about, illustrating the
important things that were

01:35:11.120 --> 01:35:12.440
discussed in the interview.

01:35:12.440 --> 01:35:14.030
And that's a great way--

01:35:14.030 --> 01:35:16.400
that's a great
sequence to do it in.

01:35:16.400 --> 01:35:18.680
Do an interview first.

01:35:18.680 --> 01:35:21.335
And then go and
think about what you

01:35:21.335 --> 01:35:23.960
need in order to tell the story
that came out in the interview.

01:35:23.960 --> 01:35:26.420
The nice thing about that
is that it doesn't kind of

01:35:26.420 --> 01:35:28.520
prejudice the interview
so that you can be very

01:35:28.520 --> 01:35:29.728
exploratory in the interview.

01:35:29.728 --> 01:35:31.490
And then you can go
and get exactly what

01:35:31.490 --> 01:35:34.883
you need to tell the story.

01:35:34.883 --> 01:35:36.300
The other way to
do it, of course,

01:35:36.300 --> 01:35:42.920
is to film the of vérité
stuff, the mobile stuff,

01:35:42.920 --> 01:35:47.030
first and then do interviews
that connect the dots

01:35:47.030 --> 01:35:48.780
to the mobile stuff.

01:35:48.780 --> 01:35:50.660
And I wanted to
show a short film

01:35:50.660 --> 01:35:56.240
that I worked on a few years
ago, which is completely

01:35:56.240 --> 01:35:59.210
shifting gears in
terms of genre.

01:35:59.210 --> 01:36:00.770
It's a mockumentary.

01:36:00.770 --> 01:36:02.670
It's a very low-budget
mockumentary,

01:36:02.670 --> 01:36:06.890
a fake documentary
that I worked on.

01:36:06.890 --> 01:36:11.540
And it stars Ed Helms.

01:36:11.540 --> 01:36:15.380
And it was done on a very, very
low budget very, very quickly.

01:36:15.380 --> 01:36:17.630
And because it was all
completely improvised,

01:36:17.630 --> 01:36:18.600
we did the opposite.

01:36:18.600 --> 01:36:20.358
We basically filmed all of the--

01:36:20.358 --> 01:36:22.400
and I'm not going to tell
you too much about it--

01:36:22.400 --> 01:36:24.338
but we filmed all the
walking around first,

01:36:24.338 --> 01:36:25.880
and then we did the
interview second.

01:36:25.880 --> 01:36:29.310
Because since he's a really
amazing improv actor,

01:36:29.310 --> 01:36:32.270
he was able to
connect all the dots.

01:36:32.270 --> 01:36:34.880
But I'm just going
to show it quickly.

01:36:34.880 --> 01:36:36.290
Then we can talk about it.

01:36:40.370 --> 01:36:42.710
And it's a profile.

01:36:42.710 --> 01:36:45.620
It's not an MIT student,
but it is a profile.

01:36:52.250 --> 01:36:54.230
I just wanted to show
that, first of all,

01:36:54.230 --> 01:37:00.950
because it was done not with
too much more time and resources

01:37:00.950 --> 01:37:02.960
than we have here.

01:37:02.960 --> 01:37:05.210
We shot it really, really
quickly in Brooklyn one day,

01:37:05.210 --> 01:37:06.830
one Sunday afternoon.

01:37:06.830 --> 01:37:11.720
And it was the opposite of what
we were just talking about.

01:37:11.720 --> 01:37:13.985
We went out with Ed and just--

01:37:13.985 --> 01:37:15.860
after he was done with
his makeup, which took

01:37:15.860 --> 01:37:18.205
about as long as the shooting--

01:37:18.205 --> 01:37:19.580
we just wandered
around Brooklyn.

01:37:19.580 --> 01:37:21.645
And we knew that we had
a couple of scenarios,

01:37:21.645 --> 01:37:23.270
like he was going to
go play basketball

01:37:23.270 --> 01:37:26.030
and that he would be
stood up on a date.

01:37:26.030 --> 01:37:29.480
But we didn't really know
what else we were going to do.

01:37:29.480 --> 01:37:31.820
And then we went back
and did an interview,

01:37:31.820 --> 01:37:33.560
which connected all the dots.

01:37:33.560 --> 01:37:38.970
And because he's an improv
actor and really good at it,

01:37:38.970 --> 01:37:43.230
he was able to tie it
all together for us.

01:37:43.230 --> 01:37:50.330
So anyway, there's just
that whole question of,

01:37:50.330 --> 01:37:51.780
how do you tell a story?

01:37:51.780 --> 01:37:53.450
What's the story
you're going to tell?

01:37:53.450 --> 01:37:55.700
This was a situation
where, again,

01:37:55.700 --> 01:37:59.240
other than the basic scenarios
and the fact that he was

01:37:59.240 --> 01:38:02.000
a zombie and that it was going
to be about the daily life

01:38:02.000 --> 01:38:06.200
struggles of a zombie done in
this kind of particular way,

01:38:06.200 --> 01:38:07.910
we didn't really have a story.

01:38:16.930 --> 01:38:22.290
So I guess the
last couple things.

01:38:22.290 --> 01:38:27.330
Just to go over again
next week, you've

01:38:27.330 --> 01:38:29.800
all done the direct
cinema exercise.

01:38:29.800 --> 01:38:33.580
The idea now is to do an
interview-based exercise,

01:38:33.580 --> 01:38:35.310
as we've been discussing.

01:38:35.310 --> 01:38:38.670
And next week, if one
person in each team

01:38:38.670 --> 01:38:41.730
could edit one or
the other, a version.

01:38:41.730 --> 01:38:46.980
Obviously, it doesn't have
to be the be-all end-all.

01:38:46.980 --> 01:38:51.075
But as completely as you
can, put together something--

01:38:51.075 --> 01:38:54.390
it doesn't have to be more
than a few minutes long--

01:38:54.390 --> 01:38:57.660
that is a best guess
at what you were

01:38:57.660 --> 01:39:01.750
trying to do with
each of the exercises.

01:39:01.750 --> 01:39:03.625
So does that make sense?

01:39:03.625 --> 01:39:04.500
Questions about that?

01:39:07.900 --> 01:39:08.400
All right.

01:39:08.400 --> 01:39:10.530
So then, just the last thing.

01:39:10.530 --> 01:39:14.400
I wanted to leave
enough time to deal

01:39:14.400 --> 01:39:16.650
with specific technical
questions that

01:39:16.650 --> 01:39:19.542
may have come up, either about
camera or sound recording.

01:39:19.542 --> 01:39:21.000
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
One other thing--

01:39:21.000 --> 01:39:22.800
unless somebody
had something else

01:39:22.800 --> 01:39:25.290
they wanted to say-- but
you had asked earlier

01:39:25.290 --> 01:39:30.240
about moving doing handheld
and moving with somebody.

01:39:30.240 --> 01:39:33.180
And then the question's
then about how

01:39:33.180 --> 01:39:35.880
do you get, if you've got
the shotgun mic on top,

01:39:35.880 --> 01:39:37.170
how do you keep getting sound?

01:39:37.170 --> 01:39:40.050
And Chris was talking
about walking backwards.

01:39:40.050 --> 01:39:42.090
So just one follow-up
technical thing on that.

01:39:42.090 --> 01:39:43.632
It's a really good
technique to learn

01:39:43.632 --> 01:39:45.180
how to do to walk backwards.

01:39:45.180 --> 01:39:46.650
I would practice first.

01:39:46.650 --> 01:39:48.800
[LAUGHS] And you
should be particularly

01:39:48.800 --> 01:39:49.800
careful with your ankle.

01:39:49.800 --> 01:39:51.883
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] part
of my physical therapy.

01:39:54.390 --> 01:39:56.640
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
also, never do it

01:39:56.640 --> 01:39:59.640
unassisted, at least if
there's any kind of question

01:39:59.640 --> 01:40:02.940
about the terrain or
bumping into something.

01:40:02.940 --> 01:40:05.400
No, seriously, you
can fall down stairs.

01:40:05.400 --> 01:40:08.326
You can break
cameras, break your--

01:40:08.326 --> 01:40:11.250
sorry-- break something on you.

01:40:11.250 --> 01:40:13.290
You just don't want to do it.

01:40:13.290 --> 01:40:16.240
Usually, what we do when
we're walking backwards is--

01:40:16.240 --> 01:40:17.880
again, you're working
in teams of two--

01:40:17.880 --> 01:40:18.960
so here.

01:40:18.960 --> 01:40:23.280
If Chris is walking backwards
with the camera, I'm here,

01:40:23.280 --> 01:40:26.370
and I'm doing this,
like OK, step,

01:40:26.370 --> 01:40:31.800
step, step, so moving
things out of the way,

01:40:31.800 --> 01:40:34.890
making sure that-- telling
her if there's something

01:40:34.890 --> 01:40:39.060
that she's going to bump into
or tapping her to move this way.

01:40:39.060 --> 01:40:41.160
And that's just something
you really want to Do.

01:40:41.160 --> 01:40:44.463
Because again, you get so
involved in the shooting,

01:40:44.463 --> 01:40:46.380
you get so involved in
the shot you're taking,

01:40:46.380 --> 01:40:49.050
that even if you know
very well that there's

01:40:49.050 --> 01:40:52.320
a table of glassware behind
you, you're almost guaranteed

01:40:52.320 --> 01:40:52.945
to run into it.

01:40:52.945 --> 01:40:55.112
CHRISTINE WALLEY: We have
some really funny footage.

01:40:55.112 --> 01:40:55.630
[LAUGHS]

01:40:55.630 --> 01:40:56.920
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh,
is this the tree?

01:40:56.920 --> 01:40:57.930
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:40:57.930 --> 01:41:00.880
We were shooting in East
Africa on this little island

01:41:00.880 --> 01:41:02.130
where I had done my fieldwork.

01:41:02.130 --> 01:41:04.150
And Chris was doing-- we
were walking up a path,

01:41:04.150 --> 01:41:05.775
and there were all
these coconut trees.

01:41:05.775 --> 01:41:08.750
And so Chris was shooting
handheld, walking backwards.

01:41:08.750 --> 01:41:10.875
And we have the sound
of like, oo-ahh,

01:41:10.875 --> 01:41:14.823
when he hits the coconut
tree and goes splat.

01:41:14.823 --> 01:41:16.740
So we actually spent a
lot of time maneuvering

01:41:16.740 --> 01:41:18.532
the coconut trees with
that particular one.

01:41:21.060 --> 01:41:23.460
So it's a really great
technique to have.

01:41:23.460 --> 01:41:27.120
And it really frees you up to be
able to do stuff more handheld,

01:41:27.120 --> 01:41:29.002
which can be really fun.

01:41:29.002 --> 01:41:30.960
Again, it's all about
what you want to capture.

01:41:30.960 --> 01:41:32.670
Do you want to do the
Errol Morris-type thing?

01:41:32.670 --> 01:41:33.962
Do you want to do the movement?

01:41:33.962 --> 01:41:38.370
But if you do want to be able
to do stuff that's more mobile,

01:41:38.370 --> 01:41:39.990
learning those
kinds of techniques,

01:41:39.990 --> 01:41:41.970
and so just practicing
them, maybe,

01:41:41.970 --> 01:41:45.020
with having a friend
spot you, is a good idea.

01:41:45.020 --> 01:41:48.210
CHRIS BOEBEL: And thinking
about how the shot changes.

01:41:48.210 --> 01:41:51.510
Again, the little thing
we just watched, actually,

01:41:51.510 --> 01:41:53.860
is a good example of this.

01:41:53.860 --> 01:41:55.140
So stand up again.

01:41:55.140 --> 01:41:59.010
So basically, I walked
all over Park Slope

01:41:59.010 --> 01:42:01.272
with Ed and Nick
and the sound guy.

01:42:01.272 --> 01:42:03.480
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Oh, so
when you were on the street?

01:42:03.480 --> 01:42:03.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

01:42:03.750 --> 01:42:05.280
So I walked backwards a lot.

01:42:05.280 --> 01:42:06.840
And Nick was--

01:42:06.840 --> 01:42:09.690
Nick Poppy, the
director-- was guiding me.

01:42:09.690 --> 01:42:14.130
But then I would come around to
the side and walk next to Ed,

01:42:14.130 --> 01:42:15.960
or I would walk behind him.

01:42:15.960 --> 01:42:18.810
He would do his thing where
he would interact with people,

01:42:18.810 --> 01:42:20.490
and I would find another angle.

01:42:20.490 --> 01:42:23.430
And Nick would know
to step out of the way

01:42:23.430 --> 01:42:25.263
and just make sure he
wasn't in the shot.

01:42:25.263 --> 01:42:27.180
And then if I started
walking backwards again,

01:42:27.180 --> 01:42:30.910
he would run in and
start guiding again.

01:42:30.910 --> 01:42:34.080
So it's just staying very
fluid and just remembering

01:42:34.080 --> 01:42:35.310
that you can change shots.

01:42:35.310 --> 01:42:39.570
You can change from one kind of
shot to another kind of shot.

01:42:39.570 --> 01:42:41.220
You can film feet.

01:42:41.220 --> 01:42:42.870
You can film cutaways.

01:42:42.870 --> 01:42:44.290
You can shoot film
point of view.

01:42:44.290 --> 01:42:45.707
So another thing
that you might do

01:42:45.707 --> 01:42:48.297
is, if you're walking
backwards, filming

01:42:48.297 --> 01:42:50.130
this person walking
through the environment,

01:42:50.130 --> 01:42:51.453
you can flip around.

01:42:51.453 --> 01:42:53.370
And all of a sudden,
you're shooting the point

01:42:53.370 --> 01:42:55.000
of view of that person.

01:42:55.000 --> 01:42:57.870
So you can shoot what's
happening on the street, what's

01:42:57.870 --> 01:43:02.880
ahead, then rotate back,
film here, go behind,

01:43:02.880 --> 01:43:08.070
go over the shoulder of
the person, film a profile.

01:43:08.070 --> 01:43:11.070
And the main thing
there, again, is

01:43:11.070 --> 01:43:13.503
to think about holding
the shots long enough so

01:43:13.503 --> 01:43:15.420
that there actually is
something there to use.

01:43:15.420 --> 01:43:17.170
If you're continually
moving and changing,

01:43:17.170 --> 01:43:18.720
you end up with nothing.

01:43:18.720 --> 01:43:22.400
It's kind of the equivalent
of hosing down the room.

01:43:22.400 --> 01:43:24.140
But definitely
don't lock yourself

01:43:24.140 --> 01:43:26.330
into one kind of angle.

01:43:26.330 --> 01:43:27.890
Try to be aware of
what's interesting

01:43:27.890 --> 01:43:28.723
and what's going on.

01:43:28.723 --> 01:43:31.238
And try to change
angles to do that.

01:43:31.238 --> 01:43:32.780
And that was one of
the things, also,

01:43:32.780 --> 01:43:35.450
that I wanted to show that
was fun about that piece.

01:43:35.450 --> 01:43:38.180
Even though it obviously
is a mockumentary,

01:43:38.180 --> 01:43:41.377
it was very much unscripted.

01:43:41.377 --> 01:43:43.460
Other than the fact that
we knew we had permission

01:43:43.460 --> 01:43:45.650
from that restaurant to
film in the restaurant

01:43:45.650 --> 01:43:48.693
at a particular time of day, we
didn't really have an agenda.

01:43:48.693 --> 01:43:50.360
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
that, in some ways,

01:43:50.360 --> 01:43:52.220
is very similar to the
Jean Rouch technique,

01:43:52.220 --> 01:43:54.110
say, in Jaguar, where
you get together,

01:43:54.110 --> 01:43:55.820
and you decide, OK, here
are certain parameters.

01:43:55.820 --> 01:43:57.528
There's a couple of
things we want to do.

01:43:57.528 --> 01:44:02.037
And then the rest of it
is improv along the way.

01:44:02.037 --> 01:44:03.620
In the last five
minutes, since people

01:44:03.620 --> 01:44:05.330
might want the
flexibility to do that,

01:44:05.330 --> 01:44:06.673
should we get out a shotgun mic?

01:44:06.673 --> 01:44:08.840
Do you want to show how to
put them onto the camera?

01:44:08.840 --> 01:44:09.888
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

01:44:09.888 --> 01:44:11.930
Is that something that
people are interested in--

01:44:11.930 --> 01:44:12.440
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Knowing how to do?

01:44:12.440 --> 01:44:13.857
CHRIS BOEBEL:
--finding out about?

01:44:13.857 --> 01:44:15.980
Because that is a great
intermediary technique.

01:44:15.980 --> 01:44:18.335
And it's definitely
better than the mic

01:44:18.335 --> 01:44:19.460
that comes with the camera.

01:44:22.080 --> 01:44:26.950
So anyway, this mic fits
into the same place.

01:44:26.950 --> 01:44:29.910
But one thing you'll notice
right away when you try it

01:44:29.910 --> 01:44:32.010
is that it's too loose.

01:44:32.010 --> 01:44:33.755
So what we usually do--

01:44:33.755 --> 01:44:35.880
we don't have it here--
but we have that blue paper

01:44:35.880 --> 01:44:39.510
tape, the painter's tape,
that you can get a roll of.

01:44:39.510 --> 01:44:42.973
And you can wrap
it around the mic.

01:44:42.973 --> 01:44:44.640
The nice thing about
that painter's tape

01:44:44.640 --> 01:44:46.015
is that it comes
off very easily.

01:44:46.015 --> 01:44:49.350
I wouldn't use masking
tape or scotch tape or tape

01:44:49.350 --> 01:44:51.035
that is hard to peel off.

01:44:51.035 --> 01:44:52.410
But that blue
painter's tape, you

01:44:52.410 --> 01:44:56.580
can wrap it around and build up
the thickness of the microphone

01:44:56.580 --> 01:44:58.878
so that it actually does fit
relatively snugly in here.

01:44:58.878 --> 01:44:59.670
Because it's close.

01:44:59.670 --> 01:45:01.193
It's just a little bit off.

01:45:01.193 --> 01:45:03.360
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And there's
painter tape in DV Lab

01:45:03.360 --> 01:45:04.767
that you can use if you want.

01:45:04.767 --> 01:45:05.850
CHRIS BOEBEL: What's that?

01:45:05.850 --> 01:45:06.450
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
I'm just telling

01:45:06.450 --> 01:45:08.280
them there's painter's tape
in DV Lab that they can use.

01:45:08.280 --> 01:45:08.610
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

01:45:08.610 --> 01:45:11.370
So if you want to do this, just
take a roll of painter's tape

01:45:11.370 --> 01:45:12.854
when you check out
your equipment.

01:45:12.854 --> 01:45:14.812
CREW: There's tons of
it, too, by the extension

01:45:14.812 --> 01:45:17.730
back there with just the
tape if you guys need some.

01:45:17.730 --> 01:45:20.160
CHRIS BOEBEL: We
got a lot, yeah.

01:45:20.160 --> 01:45:22.620
The other issue that you're
going to run into, however--

01:45:22.620 --> 01:45:26.700
and this is a little trickier--
is that on a wide angle,

01:45:26.700 --> 01:45:29.280
this sags into the frame.

01:45:29.280 --> 01:45:34.110
So especially, even
if it's built up

01:45:34.110 --> 01:45:36.360
and it's like that,
on the widest angle,

01:45:36.360 --> 01:45:37.830
this is going to
be in the frame.

01:45:37.830 --> 01:45:40.260
And you're going
to need to think

01:45:40.260 --> 01:45:41.890
of a way to deal with that.

01:45:41.890 --> 01:45:45.300
One thing that I've done,
which has a slight downside,

01:45:45.300 --> 01:45:49.980
is you can take another
piece of tape and angle

01:45:49.980 --> 01:45:51.210
this up just a little bit.

01:45:51.210 --> 01:45:53.880
Angling it up that
much is enough to have

01:45:53.880 --> 01:45:55.545
it clear the frame.

01:45:55.545 --> 01:45:57.670
But of course, now you're
pointing up a little bit.

01:45:57.670 --> 01:45:59.490
So it's you're a
little bit off axis,

01:45:59.490 --> 01:46:03.980
unless you're filming Dave,
in which case, it's perfect.

01:46:03.980 --> 01:46:06.780
So that's one thing.

01:46:06.780 --> 01:46:08.690
Other people did other things.

01:46:08.690 --> 01:46:12.260
Somebody, I think, managed
to actually secure it

01:46:12.260 --> 01:46:16.940
to the top of the
mount, like that.

01:46:16.940 --> 01:46:18.980
You just need to be
sure that it's secure.

01:46:18.980 --> 01:46:21.538
Because I don't want it,
obviously, to fall off.

01:46:21.538 --> 01:46:24.080
And the other thing is it needs
to be pretty snugly attached.

01:46:24.080 --> 01:46:26.690
Because if it does
this a lot, you're

01:46:26.690 --> 01:46:28.100
going to get sound problems.

01:46:28.100 --> 01:46:30.290
You're going to get
a lot of interference

01:46:30.290 --> 01:46:31.270
with the microphone.

01:46:31.270 --> 01:46:33.020
Because remember, it's
just like the boom.

01:46:33.020 --> 01:46:35.360
Handling noise-- the
mic shaking around,

01:46:35.360 --> 01:46:38.150
bumping into things,
hand on the boom

01:46:38.150 --> 01:46:43.568
or hand on the mic-- all of
that comes out as interference.

01:46:43.568 --> 01:46:45.110
So anyway, so that
is a potential way

01:46:45.110 --> 01:46:48.590
to do it if you can do it
securely with the tape.

01:46:48.590 --> 01:46:52.490
And that, again, is just enough
to get it out of the frame.

01:46:52.490 --> 01:46:54.960
And that way, it's
still pointing forward.

01:46:54.960 --> 01:46:57.125
So that actually is
another pretty good option.

01:47:02.370 --> 01:47:05.740
I don't know if we've ever tried
this or if it's even doable.

01:47:05.740 --> 01:47:08.100
CREW: Just cutting
into the foam itself.

01:47:08.100 --> 01:47:10.350
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, I don't
want them to cut the foam.

01:47:10.350 --> 01:47:12.150
CREW: No, I meant use
it like this could--

01:47:12.150 --> 01:47:14.400
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, I was
just going to do that, yeah.

01:47:14.400 --> 01:47:16.865
I don't know if that
actually fits, though.

01:47:16.865 --> 01:47:18.490
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Could you tape over--

01:47:18.490 --> 01:47:20.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
another option, maybe,

01:47:20.020 --> 01:47:22.510
which I haven't tried, would
be to do something like that.

01:47:22.510 --> 01:47:24.010
Bring it back just far enough.

01:47:24.010 --> 01:47:26.982
And then you can't close that.

01:47:26.982 --> 01:47:29.440
CREW: Maybe use some of the
tape to tighten it a little bit

01:47:29.440 --> 01:47:30.467
and restrict it.

01:47:30.467 --> 01:47:31.300
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

01:47:31.300 --> 01:47:35.440
Or the other thing you could
do is push the foam forward

01:47:35.440 --> 01:47:37.780
and bring it back.

01:47:37.780 --> 01:47:40.975
The only thing here, you don't
want to cover the pickups.

01:47:40.975 --> 01:47:42.850
Remember, these slots
on the sides of the mic

01:47:42.850 --> 01:47:45.640
actually are audio pickups.

01:47:45.640 --> 01:47:50.650
So you don't want to have
those in the mount like that.

01:47:50.650 --> 01:47:54.830
So you could go back right
to that point, like that.

01:47:54.830 --> 01:47:56.650
But anything further
back is going

01:47:56.650 --> 01:47:59.818
to become a problem for you.

01:47:59.818 --> 01:48:02.110
But anyway, so there is a
bit of a technical challenge.

01:48:02.110 --> 01:48:05.050
There are a couple of
ways of getting around it.

01:48:05.050 --> 01:48:09.100
But it is a way for you to
avoid that issue of the boom

01:48:09.100 --> 01:48:10.450
being so intrusive.

01:48:10.450 --> 01:48:12.250
It's more limited,
though, than the boom.

01:48:12.250 --> 01:48:14.485
Because although
you're getting a nice--

01:48:14.485 --> 01:48:20.380
so from here, I could do get
pretty decent audio of Dave.

01:48:20.380 --> 01:48:22.540
Back here, not so much.

01:48:22.540 --> 01:48:24.400
I mean, it'll be better
than the camera mic,

01:48:24.400 --> 01:48:27.610
but it's not going
to be fantastic.

01:48:27.610 --> 01:48:30.970
And of course, I also
can't come over the top.

01:48:30.970 --> 01:48:32.120
I don't have any reach.

01:48:32.120 --> 01:48:36.150
So with the boom, I can be
back here, filming you two.

01:48:36.150 --> 01:48:37.900
And then the boom
person can be like this.

01:48:37.900 --> 01:48:41.110
But of course, you two
may be very ill at ease

01:48:41.110 --> 01:48:43.030
with this thing
hanging over your head.

01:48:43.030 --> 01:48:44.680
So it's a trade-off.

01:48:44.680 --> 01:48:47.710
CREW: The benefit of doing
the 45 degree angle from above

01:48:47.710 --> 01:48:50.290
is that you don't
get the ambient noise

01:48:50.290 --> 01:48:51.610
behind the person--

01:48:51.610 --> 01:48:53.350
well, not as much of it, anyway.

01:48:53.350 --> 01:48:54.520
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
again, remember, these

01:48:54.520 --> 01:48:55.395
are very directional.

01:48:55.395 --> 01:48:58.480
So if I'm filming
Dave on a loading dock

01:48:58.480 --> 01:49:01.000
and there's a truck
idling behind him,

01:49:01.000 --> 01:49:04.330
I'm going to pick up the
truck really, really well.

01:49:04.330 --> 01:49:08.440
If I'm coming in like this,
I'm not, really, so much.

01:49:08.440 --> 01:49:10.630
Because the pattern is down.

01:49:10.630 --> 01:49:13.330
But I'm still going
to hear his sound.

01:49:13.330 --> 01:49:16.630
So that's another consideration.

01:49:16.630 --> 01:49:19.780
But this is something that we've
done very successfully that's

01:49:19.780 --> 01:49:20.710
less obtrusive.

01:49:20.710 --> 01:49:22.970
And it's definitely worth
knowing and thinking about.

01:49:22.970 --> 01:49:26.880
And it's a good technique for
certain kinds of situations.

